window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2010"] = {"id": "jammu-2010", "title": "University of Jammu 2010", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance 2010, booklet code ELW-6733 (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2010 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following is currently outside general psychology?", "options": {"a": "Physiological Psychology", "b": "Psychophysics", "c": "Aviation Psychology", "d": "Industrial Psychology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: aviation psychology is the field that lies outside general psychology. General psychology is the core course that teaches the basic principles of behaviour and mental processes shared by everyone: sensation, perception, attention, learning, memory, motivation, emotion, intelligence and personality. Two basic fields sit inside it. Physiological psychology studies the biological bases of behaviour (the nervous system, glands and hormones). Psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), measures how the physical intensity of a stimulus relates to the sensation it produces, and it became the foundation of experimental psychology. Applied fields, by contrast, carry these principles into one particular setting. Aviation psychology is a narrow applied speciality: selecting and training pilots, designing cockpits and instruments, and studying fatigue and human error in flight. It grew out of pilot selection in the First and Second World Wars. Example: Arjun meets thresholds and the nervous system in general psychology, but pilot selection only in a specialised aviation course. Option c is right because aviation psychology applies psychology to one occupation rather than describing basic processes common to all people. Exam trap: examiners pair a narrow applied speciality with a broad, familiar applied branch, so the rule \"any applied field is outside\" makes both c and d look right. Key note: industrial psychology is also an applied field, but it is a broad, long-established branch (Hugo Munsterberg, Psychology and Industrial Efficiency, 1913) that most general psychology courses still introduce; aviation psychology is the one that sits clearly outside, so c stands.", "topic": "Fields of psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "While a Psychologist emphasizes human behaviour, Psychology stresses:", "options": {"a": "Human Motivation", "b": "Emotional behaviour", "c": "Individuality", "d": "Cognitive growth"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychology stresses individuality. The stem is garbled in print, but it is the standard textbook contrast between sociology and psychology. Sociology studies human groups, society and its institutions (family, caste, religion, the state): how people behave as members of groups and how social structures shape them. A classic example is Emile Durkheim's Suicide (1897), which explained differences in suicide rates between groups by how well each group integrated its members. Psychology studies the individual: one person's behaviour and mental processes, and the individual differences in ability, temperament and personality that make each person unique. Both disciplines study human behaviour; they differ in the unit of analysis. Social psychology sits between them, asking how the individual is influenced by other people. Example: a sociologist might compare exam stress across states and income groups, while a psychologist asks why Meera, but not her classmate, panics before every test. Option c is right because individuality is the distinguishing emphasis of psychology as against sociology. Exam trap: motivation, emotion and cognitive growth are all real topics within psychology, so they look attractive; but they are subject areas inside the discipline, not the feature that sets psychology apart from another discipline. Key note: the paper prints \"While a Psychologist emphasizes human behaviour\"; it almost certainly meant \"While a sociologist emphasises group behaviour\". Read that way, c is the only option that contrasts psychology with another discipline, so c stands.", "topic": "Psychology versus sociology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Besides Heredity and Environment which of the following factors is essentially concerned with growth and development?", "options": {"a": "Gender", "b": "Climate", "c": "Nature", "d": "Time"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: time is the third factor, besides heredity and environment, that governs growth and development. Heredity is the set of potentials passed on through the genes at conception; environment is everything that acts on the person from outside, from the womb onwards (nutrition, family, school, culture). Many texts write the relation as Development = f(Heredity x Environment x Time). Time matters for two reasons. First, growth unfolds over time through maturation, the biological ripening of the body and nervous system on a genetic timetable, so a child cannot walk before the body is ready. Second, the same experience has different effects at different ages: there are critical and sensitive periods when the organism is most open to certain influences, such as language in early childhood, or the damage a drug can do in the first weeks of pregnancy. Example: Kabir, adopted abroad at six months, speaks the new language with a native accent, while his cousin who moved at fifteen keeps a foreign one; same environment, different timing. Option d is right because time adds a separate dimension, the age and timing at which heredity and environment interact. Exam trap: \"nature\" looks like a new factor, but it is only another word for heredity (the nature and nurture phrase popularised by Francis Galton, 1874), so it repeats the stem. Key note: many texts name only heredity and environment as the two factors; among the options only time adds a genuinely separate factor, so d stands.", "topic": "Determinants of development", "fig": ""}, {"q": "CR is contingent upon:", "options": {"a": "CS", "b": "UCS", "c": "UCR", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the conditioned response (CR) is contingent upon the conditioned stimulus (CS). In the classical conditioning described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), food in a dog's mouth is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), which elicits salivation, the unconditioned response (UCR), without any learning. A neutral sound, such as a metronome or bell, is presented just before the food many times. After this pairing the sound alone produces salivation: the sound has become the CS, and salivation to it is the CR. The CR is defined by its link to the CS. It appears only when the CS is presented, and it weakens (extinction) if the CS keeps appearing without the UCS. Robert Rescorla (1968) sharpened the idea: conditioning depends on the contingency between CS and UCS, that is, on the CS reliably predicting the UCS, not on mere pairing in time. Example: Riya's mouth waters when she hears the pressure cooker whistle, because the whistle has always come just before lunch; the whistle is the CS and her watering mouth is the CR. Option a is right because the CR is, by definition, the learned response triggered by the CS. Exam trap: the UCS is tempting, since conditioning cannot happen without it during learning; but the UCS elicits the UCR, while the CR, whenever it occurs, depends on the CS. Key note: the CR is learned only because the CS was paired with the UCS, and it extinguishes when the UCS stops; but what the CR is contingent on when it occurs is the CS, so a stands.", "topic": "Classical conditioning", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Cognitive mapping is linked with:", "options": {"a": "Insight Learning", "b": "Conditioning", "c": "Imitative Learning", "d": "Intuitive learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive mapping is linked with insight learning, the other classic cognitive form of learning. Edward Tolman (1948), in \"Cognitive maps in rats and men\", argued that rats learn a mental map of a maze rather than a chain of turns. In the latent learning study of Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930), rats that explored a maze unrewarded for ten days matched the always-rewarded rats within a day or two of food being introduced: they had learned the layout all along. Tolman called his view purposive behaviourism and drew on Gestalt ideas. Insight learning comes from Wolfgang Kohler (1925), whose chimpanzee Sultan, playing idly with two short sticks, fitted them together and at once used them to rake in a banana out of reach. Both treat learning as a mental reorganisation of the relations in a situation, not as stimulus-response links stamped in by reward. Example: when her usual road is closed, Meera at once takes a new lane home, because she carries a map of the area in her head; that is cognitive learning, not a drilled habit. Option a is right because cognitive maps and insight both belong to the cognitive, Gestalt-influenced view of learning. Exam trap: Tolman ran rats in mazes, the behaviourists' own apparatus, so conditioning looks right; but his point was that rats learn more than S-R habits. Key note: the textbook pair for cognitive maps is latent learning, which is not an option; among the options, insight learning is the other classic cognitive (non S-R) form of learning, so a stands.", "topic": "Cognitive maps and insight", "fig": "Edward Tolman"}, {"q": "According to Ebbinghaus (1885) memory is a function of:", "options": {"a": "Motivation", "b": "Time", "c": "Maturation", "d": "Interest"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) showed that memory is a function of time, the interval since learning. Working alone and testing himself, he learned lists of nonsense syllables (consonant-vowel-consonant trigrams such as DAX or BUP), chosen precisely because they carried no meaning, interest or earlier associations. He learned each list until he could recite it twice without error, relearned it to the same point after a set delay, and measured savings: how much less time relearning took. Plotting savings against delay gave the forgetting curve. Forgetting is fastest at first and then slows: savings fell to about 44 percent after an hour and about 34 percent after a day, then levelled off over the following month without reaching zero. Retention, in short, changes lawfully with the time elapsed. He also found that repetitions spread over several days work better than the same number massed together. Example: Arjun learns twenty new vocabulary words at 9 a.m.; most of what he will forget goes in the first hour or so, and far less over the following days. Option b is right because time since learning was the variable Ebbinghaus manipulated, and retention depended systematically on it. Exam trap: interest and motivation clearly affect everyday memory, so they look plausible; but Ebbinghaus removed them on purpose by using meaningless material, which is exactly why time stood out as the variable.", "topic": "Ebbinghaus forgetting curve", "fig": "Hermann Ebbinghaus"}, {"q": "Conflict analysis constitutes the work of:", "options": {"a": "Kurt Lewin", "b": "Maslow", "c": "Kohler", "d": "Guthrie"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: conflict analysis is the work of Kurt Lewin (1935). In his field theory, set out in A Dynamic Theory of Personality, behaviour results from forces in the person's life space: the person plus the psychological environment as he or she sees it. Goals have valence; a positive valence attracts and a negative valence repels. A conflict arises when forces of roughly equal strength pull in opposite directions. Lewin described three basic types. In approach-approach conflict there are two attractive goals and only one can be chosen; it is the easiest to resolve. In avoidance-avoidance conflict both options are unpleasant, and the person vacillates or tries to leave the field. In approach-avoidance conflict one goal both attracts and repels, so the person vacillates near the goal; it is often the hardest to settle. Neal Miller (1944) later measured approach and avoidance gradients in rats, and a fourth type, double approach-avoidance, was added. Example: Kabir wins a seat at a top college far from home; he wants the college but dreads leaving his family, a classic approach-avoidance conflict. Option a is right because the systematic analysis of conflict types in terms of valences and forces comes from Lewin's field theory. Exam trap: Wolfgang Kohler (1925) is the tempting name, because Lewin worked alongside him in the Berlin Gestalt group; but Kohler's work was insight, not conflict.", "topic": "Lewin's conflict types", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "For two factor theory of intelligence if factor 1 is \"S\" which is the other one?", "options": {"a": "Wf", "b": "Sp", "c": "G", "d": "Mc"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the other factor in Charles Spearman's two-factor theory is G, the general factor. Spearman (1904) invented factor analysis to explain a striking finding: schoolchildren's marks in very different subjects, and their scores on sensory tasks, all correlated positively (the positive manifold). He proposed that every intellectual task draws on two kinds of ability. The general factor, g, is common to all tasks; Spearman thought of it as a kind of mental energy, and it showed most strongly in reasoning tasks. Each task also needs its own specific factor, s, peculiar to that task alone, which is why the correlations between tests are high but never perfect. A person's score on any test therefore reflects g plus that test's s. He set the theory out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya does well in mathematics, Hindi and music; her general factor runs through all three, while her special flair for rhythm is an s factor for music. Option c is right because Spearman's theory has exactly two factors, g and s. Exam trap: the other options look like abbreviations from the later multiple-factor and hierarchical models, which added group factors between g and s; Spearman's original theory had no group factors at all.", "topic": "Spearman's two-factor theory", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Behaviourists tried to reduce emotions to:", "options": {"a": "Mentalistic concepts", "b": "Sensory responses", "c": "Behavioural Frames", "d": "Excitatory behaviours"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: behaviourists tried to reduce emotions to behavioural frames, that is, observable patterns of bodily reaction. John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism by rejecting introspection: psychology should study only what can be observed and measured. Applied to emotion, this meant dropping inner feelings and describing emotion as a pattern of response. In Psychology from the Standpoint of a Behaviorist (1919) Watson defined an emotion as \"an hereditary 'pattern-reaction' involving profound changes of the bodily mechanism as a whole, but particularly of the visceral and glandular systems\". He held that infants show only three unlearned emotional patterns: fear (to a loud noise or sudden loss of support), rage (to hampered movement) and love (to stroking). All other emotions, he argued, are conditioned from these, as Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) tried to show by conditioning fear of a white rat in Little Albert. Example: a behaviourist would describe Arjun's fear of dogs not as a feeling but as trembling, a racing heart and running away when a dog barks. Option c is right because behaviourists described emotions purely as frames or patterns of observable behaviour. Exam trap: mentalistic concepts sound scientific, but they are exactly what behaviourists threw out. Key note: \"excitatory behaviours\" echoes the later activation (arousal) view of emotion (Elizabeth Duffy, 1941; Donald Lindsley, 1951), which is close but not the classic behaviourist account; Watson's pattern-reactions are behavioural patterns, so c stands.", "topic": "Behaviourist view of emotion", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "In Maslow's scheme of motivation basal needs are shared by:", "options": {"a": "Both humans and animals", "b": "Humans only", "c": "Animals only", "d": "None of the above categories"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Abraham Maslow's (1943) scheme, the basal needs are shared by both humans and animals. Maslow arranged human motives in a hierarchy, set out fully in Motivation and Personality (1954): physiological needs (hunger, thirst, sleep, sex), safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation at the top. By prepotency, an unmet lower need dominates behaviour until it is largely satisfied, after which higher needs take charge. The basal (lower) needs, physiological and safety, are biological, and they are plainly present in animals too: a dog seeks food, water and shelter and avoids danger. Maslow called the basic needs instinctoid, weakly rooted in our biology. He also argued that the higher a need sits, the more distinctly human it is: we share the need for food with all living things, the need for love perhaps with the higher apes, and the need for self-actualisation with no other species. Example: when Meera has skipped lunch before an exam, her hunger, a need any animal has, pushes aside her wish to impress the examiner. Option a is right because the lower physiological and safety needs are common to humans and animals. Exam trap: because the hierarchy is a theory of human motivation, many pick \"humans only\"; but that description fits the higher needs, not the basal ones.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "Individual space concept is core to:", "options": {"a": "Survey method", "b": "Interview", "c": "Sociometry", "d": "Observation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the concept of individual (personal) space is core to the observation method. Personal space is the invisible, portable zone around a person's body that others are not supposed to enter; it shrinks with intimacy and varies with culture, sex and situation. Robert Sommer (1959), in \"Studies in personal space\", and Edward T. Hall (1966), in The Hidden Dimension, made it a research topic. Hall's proxemics described four zones for Americans: intimate (up to 18 inches), personal (18 inches to 4 feet), social (4 to 12 feet) and public (beyond 12 feet). Because people are largely unaware of the distances they keep, they cannot report them reliably, so researchers watch and measure behaviour instead. They record how far apart people sit or stand, and what happens when someone intrudes: Nancy Felipe and Robert Sommer (1966) sat uncomfortably close to people in a mental hospital and a university library and recorded how quickly they moved away. Example: in a nearly empty library, an observer notes that when Kabir sits right beside a stranger, she turns away, builds a wall of books and soon leaves. Option d is right because personal space is studied by observing actual spacing behaviour. Exam trap: sociometry sounds like a measure of social distance, but it maps who chooses whom, not physical distance. Key note: Sommer's 1959 paper appeared in the journal Sociometry, which may have prompted c, but sociometry the method measures social choice, not space; personal space is a matter of observed behaviour, so d stands.", "topic": "Personal space and observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sociology is defined as \"a science which interprets social behaviour with the aim of arriving at a causal explanation of human behaviour\". Whose statement is this?", "options": {"a": "Comte", "b": "Weber", "c": "Simmel", "d": "Jones"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the definition is Max Weber's (1922). In Economy and Society, published after his death, Weber wrote that sociology is a science which attempts the interpretive understanding of social action in order to arrive at a causal explanation of its course and effects. Two ideas sit in that sentence. Social action is behaviour to which the actor attaches a meaning and which takes account of other people. Interpretive understanding, Verstehen, means grasping that meaning from the actor's point of view, rather than only counting outward events. Weber then insisted that this understanding must lead to causal explanation, so that sociology remains a science and not mere description. His approach is called interpretive sociology. Example: to explain why Riya's family chooses an arranged marriage, a Weberian sociologist first asks what the choice means to them (duty, family honour, security) and then explains how such shared meanings produce the wider social pattern. Option b is right because interpretive understanding leading to causal explanation is the core of Weber's definition. Exam trap: Auguste Comte (1838) is the reflex answer because he coined the word sociology, but his positivism sought social laws through observation, not the interpretation of meaning.", "topic": "Weber's definition of sociology", "fig": "Max Weber"}, {"q": "Most important characteristic of a group leader is:", "options": {"a": "Skill at job", "b": "Intelligence", "c": "Access to resources", "d": "Ability to manipulate people"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: among these options, intelligence is the characteristic most consistently linked with leadership. The trait approach, or great-person theory, asked which personal qualities set leaders apart. Ralph Stogdill (1948) reviewed 124 studies and found that leaders tended to exceed other members in intelligence, alertness, dependability, sociability and activity, though no single trait guaranteed leadership in every situation. Robert Lord, Christy De Vader and George Alliger (1986) re-analysed the evidence by meta-analysis and found intelligence to be the strongest trait correlate of who is perceived as a leader. Intelligence helps a leader understand the task, solve problems, judge situations and plan for the group. Texts add that leaders are usually brighter than their followers, but not by too wide a margin, since a large gap can hamper communication. Example: in a college project group, members turn to Arjun, who grasps the problem quickly and explains it clearly, rather than to the student who controls the budget. Option b is right because intelligence is the personal trait that research repeatedly ties to leadership. Exam trap: access to resources sounds powerful, but it is a source of power attached to a position, not a characteristic of the leader as a person. Key note: Stogdill also showed no single trait guarantees leadership and the situation matters, but among these options intelligence is the trait the research singles out, so b stands.", "topic": "Leadership traits", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Whose study demonstrated the influence of a leader on a formal group?", "options": {"a": "Hollander", "b": "Ginsberg", "c": "Torrance", "d": "Julian"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the study by E. Paul Torrance (1954) of air force bomber crews demonstrated a leader's influence on a formal group. A formal group has an official structure of positions, ranks and roles set by an organisation. Torrance, the same psychologist who later built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, was then working for the United States Air Force and studied three-man B-26 bomber crews, whose hierarchy was fixed: the pilot (the commander) at the top, the navigator next and the gunner at the bottom. When crews solved problems together, the group decision followed the pilot's view most often, even when the pilot was wrong, while the low-status gunner was least able to get a correct answer accepted. The pattern held even on tasks that had nothing to do with flying, and rank mattered less in temporary crews put together only for the study. His report, \"Some Consequences of Power Differences on Decisions in B-26 Crews\" (1954), showed that formal status and power shape group decisions. Example: in an office team, Meera the junior clerk spots an error in the figures, but the group follows the manager's answer anyway. Option c is right because Torrance's crews showed how the formal leader's position governs group outcomes. Exam trap: Edwin Hollander (1958) is the famous leadership name, so he is the reflex choice; but his work concerns emergent leaders and idiosyncrasy credits, not the influence of rank in formal crews.", "topic": "Leaders in formal groups", "fig": "E. Paul Torrance"}, {"q": "In which communication network is the leader the only person who can talk to any member of the group, while no one else can?", "options": {"a": "Circle network", "b": "Wheel network", "c": "Chain network", "d": "Y-shape network"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the wheel network the leader at the centre is the only person who can talk to every member, while the others can talk only to the leader. Communication networks are fixed patterns of who may send messages to whom. Alex Bavelas (1950) and Harold Leavitt (1951) studied them by placing five people behind partitions, letting them pass written notes only along set channels, and giving them a shared problem. In the wheel, the most centralised network, every message passes through the hub. On simple tasks it is the fastest and most accurate, and the hub quickly emerges as leader, but members on the spokes are the least satisfied. In the chain, messages pass along a line and the middle person relays. The Y is a chain with a fork at one end, fairly centralised. In the circle each person talks only to two neighbours: slow, with no clear leader, but the highest morale. Later work added the all-channel network, in which everyone talks to everyone. Example: in a tuition centre, Kabir the coordinator receives every teacher's report and passes on every instruction, and the teachers never talk to each other: a wheel. Option b is right because only the wheel gives one central person access to all and denies it to everyone else. Exam trap: the Y network also has a central position, but there other members still have links of their own; only in the wheel does every message go through one person.", "topic": "Communication networks", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Most important works on attitudes were carried out by:", "options": {"a": "Edwards", "b": "Likert", "c": "Thurstone", "d": "Park"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the most important early work on attitudes was carried out by Louis Leon Thurstone. In a famous paper titled \"Attitudes can be measured\" (1928), Thurstone argued that an attitude, a person's favourable or unfavourable evaluation of an object, could be placed on a numerical scale like any other quantity. With Ernest J. Chave in The Measurement of Attitude (1929), measuring attitude towards the church, he devised the method of equal-appearing intervals. In that study 300 judges sorted 130 opinion statements into 11 piles, from most unfavourable to most favourable. Each statement's scale value was its median pile; statements the judges placed inconsistently were dropped, and 45 evenly graded statements formed the final scale. A respondent ticks the statements he or she agrees with, and the score is the average (median or mean) scale value of those ticked. This was the first serious attempt to measure attitudes, and it opened the whole field of attitude scaling. Example: Riya's score on a Thurstone scale of attitude towards women's education is the average scale value of the statements she endorses. Option c is right because Thurstone pioneered attitude measurement and the other names built on his foundation. Exam trap: the summated rating scale of Rensis Likert (1932) is the one used everywhere today, so it feels more important. Key note: Likert's scale is more widely used today, but Thurstone's work is the foundational one that first showed attitudes could be measured, so c stands.", "topic": "Thurstone and attitude measurement", "fig": "Louis Leon Thurstone"}, {"q": "Likert-scale is normally a:", "options": {"a": "Five-valued measure", "b": "Seven-valued measure", "c": "Three-valued measure", "d": "Two-valued measure"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: a Likert scale is normally a five-valued (five-point) measure. Rensis Likert (1932), in \"A technique for the measurement of attitudes\", introduced the method of summated ratings. The researcher writes a set of opinion statements about the attitude object, some favourable and some unfavourable. Each respondent rates every statement on five ordered alternatives: strongly agree, agree, undecided, disagree, strongly disagree. Responses are scored 5 to 1 for favourable statements and reversed (1 to 5) for unfavourable ones, so that a high total always means a favourable attitude. The item scores are then added with equal weights, hence \"summated\". No panel of judges is needed, unlike the method of Louis Leon Thurstone (1928); items are kept by item analysis if high and low total scorers answer them differently. Likert scales are easy to build and usually as reliable as Thurstone scales, which is why they dominate questionnaires. Example: Arjun marks \"agree\" (scored 4) on \"Exams motivate me to study\" and \"strongly disagree\" on \"Exams are a waste of time\", which reverses to 5. Option a is right because the standard Likert format has five response points. Exam trap: seven-point versions are common in modern research, so b looks plausible; but the classic, textbook Likert item is five-point.", "topic": "Likert scale format", "fig": "Rensis Likert"}, {"q": "Which one do you consider most important for determining interpersonal attraction?", "options": {"a": "Similarity of interests", "b": "Proximity", "c": "Complementarity", "d": "Rewardingness"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: proximity, physical nearness, is the factor texts call most important in determining who becomes attracted to whom. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) studied Westgate West, married-student housing at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where flats were assigned at random. Residents named 41 percent of their next-door neighbours as close friends, against 22 percent of those two doors away and only 10 percent of those at the far end of the corridor. Functional distance mattered too: people living near stairways and mailboxes made more friends. Proximity works because it brings repeated exposure, and Robert Zajonc (1968) showed through the mere exposure effect that familiarity alone raises liking; it also gives people the chance to interact, discover similarities and exchange rewards. Without some contact, no other factor can operate. Example: Kabir's closest friends in his first hostel year are the boys on his own floor, not the ones he would have picked by interests. Option b is right because proximity is the first and most powerful precondition for attraction. Exam trap: similarity is a strong predictor once people have met (Donn Byrne, 1971), so it is the popular wrong choice. Key note: similarity and reward are close rivals, and reward theory explains why the others work; but texts usually call proximity the single most powerful predictor of who becomes friends, since attraction cannot start without contact, so b stands.", "topic": "Proximity and attraction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one is the most populated country in the world?", "options": {"a": "USA", "b": "Russia", "c": "China", "d": "India"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: China was the most populous country in the world in 2010, the year this question dates from. China's sixth national census, taken in November 2010, counted about 1.34 billion people on the mainland. India was second: its 2011 census counted about 1.21 billion. The United States, third, had about 309 million at its 2010 census, and Russia about 143 million, so neither came close. China's growth had slowed sharply after the one-child policy of around 1980, while India's population kept growing faster because of higher fertility, so the gap between the two was steadily closing. Population size matters to psychologists and social scientists because density and crowding affect stress, health and social behaviour, and population pressure shapes education, employment and migration. Example: when Meera studied geography in 2010, her textbook listed China first and India a close second, with India expected to pass China within a few decades. Option c is right because, in 2010, China's population exceeded that of every other country. Exam trap: India is the right answer to an up-to-date question, so anyone who follows recent news may pick it for this old item. Key note: this answer is correct for 2010. The United Nations (World Population Prospects 2022) estimated that India overtook China around April 2023, so if this item appears in a current exam the answer is India; for this 2010 paper, c stands.", "topic": "World population ranking", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The major agenda of the Copenhagen conference 2009 was:", "options": {"a": "Population explosion", "b": "Environmental degradation", "c": "Industrial Emission Control", "d": "Global Warming"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the major agenda of the Copenhagen conference of December 2009 was global warming. The meeting was COP15, the 15th Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC, adopted in 1992). Its aim was to agree a successor to the Kyoto Protocol (1997), whose first commitment period ran to 2012, with binding cuts in the greenhouse gases (carbon dioxide, methane and others) that trap heat in the atmosphere and raise global temperatures. The talks did not produce a binding treaty. Instead, a small group of countries, including the United States, China, India, Brazil and South Africa, drafted the Copenhagen Accord, which the conference only \"took note\" of. The Accord recognised that warming should be kept below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels, asked countries to pledge emission targets, and promised climate finance for developing countries. Example: Arjun, writing a newspaper summary, would headline the meeting as a climate change summit, not a population or pollution conference. Option d is right because the conference was held under the climate change convention to tackle global warming. Exam trap: environmental degradation and industrial emission control both sound right because they are related; but the first is far broader than the agenda, and the second is only one means to the climate goal.", "topic": "Copenhagen climate summit 2009", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which school of psychology focuses on psychodynamics?", "options": {"a": "Behavioural", "b": "Gestalt", "c": "Psychoanalytic", "d": "Humanistic"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychodynamics is the central idea of the psychoanalytic school. Psychodynamics means the study of mental forces in motion: unconscious wishes, drives and conflicts that push against each other and against the demands of reality and conscience, while defence mechanisms keep the struggle out of awareness. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) built psychoanalysis on this idea. He pictured the mind as id, ego and superego in constant tension, and he held that symptoms, slips of the tongue and dreams are compromises between a forbidden wish and the forces that block it. Example: Kabir keeps 'forgetting' his dentist appointment; a psychodynamic reading says an unconscious fear is quietly winning over his conscious intention. Followers and rivals who kept this dynamic view, such as Alfred Adler and Carl Jung (who broke with Freud between 1911 and 1913), the ego psychologists and the object relations theorists, are grouped together as the psychodynamic approach. Option c is right because psychoanalysis is the only school whose core concern is the dynamic play of unconscious forces. The other schools explain behaviour without inner conflict: behaviourism through learned stimulus-response links, Gestalt psychology through the organisation of perception, and humanistic psychology through growth toward self-actualisation. Exam trap: the word 'dynamic' also appears in Kurt Lewin's (1935) A Dynamic Theory of Personality and in Robert Woodworth's (1918) Dynamic Psychology, so it is easy to link psychodynamics to Gestalt or field theory; the term psychodynamics belongs to Freud's tradition.", "topic": "Psychoanalysis and psychodynamics", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Human behaviour is largely regulated by:", "options": {"a": "Central nervous system", "b": "Autonomic nervous system", "c": "Sensory stimulation", "d": "Parasympathetic nervous system"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: human behaviour is largely regulated by the central nervous system. The nervous system has two main parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is all the nerves outside them; it divides into the somatic system (sensory nerves carrying messages in, motor nerves carrying commands out to the skeletal muscles) and the autonomic nervous system (ANS), which serves the internal organs. The CNS is the control centre. It receives sensory messages, integrates them with memory, motives and plans, and sends out the commands that produce movement, speech, thought and emotional expression. The spinal cord handles simple reflexes and relays messages; the brain does the higher work. Even the ANS is steered by the CNS, mainly through the hypothalamus and the brainstem. Example: when Meera hears her name called on a crowded bus, her ears send signals inward, her cortex recognises her friend's voice, and her brain directs her to turn and answer; every step is organised centrally. Option a is right because the brain and spinal cord integrate input and direct nearly all behaviour, voluntary and involuntary. Exam trap: the ANS sounds 'autonomous', so it is easy to think it runs behaviour on its own; in fact it controls only internal organs such as the heart, glands and gut, and its two branches, sympathetic and parasympathetic, are themselves regulated by the brain.", "topic": "Central nervous system and behaviour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Inferiority complex is a biosocial phenomenon\": who said this?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Adler", "c": "Jung", "d": "Fromm"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Alfred Adler described the inferiority complex as a biosocial phenomenon. Alfred Adler (1907, Study of Organ Inferiority) founded individual psychology after leaving Sigmund Freud's circle in 1911. He began with the body: a weak organ (poor eyesight, a stammer, a frail chest) makes a person feel inferior and drives him to compensate, sometimes so well that the weakness becomes a strength. He then widened the idea. Every child starts small, dependent and less capable than the adults around him, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. How these feelings develop depends on social life: comparison with siblings and peers, birth order, pampering or neglect, and the growth of social interest. Because the roots are both biological (the body, childhood helplessness) and social (family, comparison with others), the phenomenon is biosocial. When inferiority feelings become exaggerated and paralysing, they form an inferiority complex; covering them with arrogance is the superiority complex. Healthy striving for superiority, guided by social interest, is the normal way out. Example: Arjun, short and teased at school, works hard at debating and becomes the school's best speaker; that is compensation. Option b is right because the inferiority concept, its bodily origin and its social shaping are all Adler's. Exam trap: the word 'complex' is easily linked with Carl Jung, who used it for clusters of emotionally charged ideas found in his word association tests; but the inferiority complex is Adler's.", "topic": "Adler: inferiority complex", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "C. G. Jung proposed the theory of:", "options": {"a": "Unconscious", "b": "Collective Unconscious", "c": "Subconscious", "d": "Ethical Unconscious"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Carl Gustav Jung proposed the theory of the collective unconscious. Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961), a Swiss psychiatrist, was Sigmund Freud's closest early ally but broke with him in 1913 and founded analytical psychology. Jung kept Freud's idea of a personal unconscious, the layer that holds each person's forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes. Beneath it he placed a deeper, inherited layer common to all humanity: the collective unconscious. It contains archetypes, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in typical ways, which appear in myths, dreams, religions and folk tales across cultures. Key archetypes are the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, rejected side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in men and the masculine side in women), the great mother, the wise old man and the self (the archetype of wholeness). So Jung's psyche has three layers: the conscious ego, the personal unconscious and the collective unconscious. Example: Riya dreams of a wise old guide on a mountain, a figure she has never met; Jung would read the figure as an archetype rising from the collective unconscious, not as a personal memory. Option b is right because the collective unconscious is Jung's distinctive contribution. Exam trap: option a looks right because Jung wrote a great deal about the unconscious, but the theory of the unconscious as such is Freud's; only the collective, inherited layer is Jung's.", "topic": "Jung: collective unconscious", "fig": "Carl Gustav Jung"}, {"q": "16PF scale is based on the views of:", "options": {"a": "Allport", "b": "Eysenck", "c": "Cattell", "d": "Rogers"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the 16PF scale is based on the trait theory of Raymond Cattell. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) wanted to find the basic units of personality scientifically rather than by intuition. He began with Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert's (1936) list of 17,953 trait words from the dictionary, kept the roughly 4,500 that name stable traits, merged synonyms into 171 terms, and then applied factor analysis, a statistical method that finds which measures vary together. He used three kinds of data: L-data (life records and ratings by others), Q-data (self-report questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests). The result was 16 source traits, the building blocks of personality, published as the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) in 1949. The factors include warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, liveliness, rule-consciousness, social boldness, sensitivity, vigilance, abstractedness, privateness, apprehension, openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism and tension. Example: Kabir's 16PF profile shows high dominance and low apprehension; his counsellor reads the 16 scores as a map of his source traits when advising him on careers. Option c is right because the 16PF is Cattell's own factor-analytic inventory. Exam trap: Gordon Allport also worked with trait words, and Cattell started from Allport's list, so a is tempting; but Allport preferred studying the unique individual, distrusted factor analysis and never built the 16PF.", "topic": "Cattell: the 16PF", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "\"Central\" and \"Surface\" traits are the components of:", "options": {"a": "Allport's system", "b": "Freud's system", "c": "Adler's system", "d": "Cattell's system"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: surface and central (source) traits are the components of Raymond Cattell's system. Raymond Cattell (1950, Personality: A Systematic Theoretical and Factual Study) separated two levels of traits. Surface traits are clusters of behaviours that tend to go together and can be seen directly, such as a person who is helpful, polite, generous and patient. They are many and fairly unstable. Source traits are the deeper, central underlying factors that cause the surface clusters; they can be found only by factor analysis, they are stable, and the 16PF measures 16 of them. Cattell also classed traits by content: ability traits, temperament traits and dynamic traits (ergs, or innate drives, and learned sentiments). Example: Meera's friends see her as talkative, cheerful and fond of parties; these surface behaviours stem from a single source trait, which Cattell would call high liveliness and, at the second-order level, extraversion. Option d is right because the surface versus source distinction is unique to Cattell, and the 'central' traits in the stem can only mean his source traits, the core factors beneath the surface. Exam trap: Gordon Allport (1937) used the term 'central traits' for the 5 to 10 traits that best describe a person, alongside cardinal and secondary traits, so a looks tempting. Key note: 'central' is Allport's word, so the stem mixes the two systems; but surface traits belong only to Cattell, whose source traits are the central, underlying ones, so d stands.", "topic": "Cattell: surface and source traits", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Abnormality is primarily a:", "options": {"a": "Qualitative disequilibrium", "b": "Quantitative extremism", "c": "Mental derailment", "d": "Conscious diffusion"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: abnormality is primarily a matter of quantitative extremism. The continuity view, also called the dimensional or statistical view, holds that normal and abnormal behaviour differ in degree, not in kind. Everyone has some anxiety, sadness, suspicion and fussiness about order; abnormality is the same quality pushed to an extreme, too intense, too frequent or too long-lasting. On the normal curve most people cluster around the average, and the statistical criterion calls behaviour abnormal when it lies far out in the tails, for example beyond two standard deviations from the mean. Example: Kabir checks the door lock once before leaving, as most people do; his cousin checks it forty times and is late every day. The behaviour is the same; only the amount differs, and the extreme amount is what makes it abnormal. This view underlies rating scales for anxiety and depression and the dimensional model of personality disorders in Section III of DSM-5 (2013). Option b is right because the standard teaching treats abnormality as an extreme position on dimensions shared by everyone. Exam trap: option a sounds scholarly, but 'qualitative' means different in kind, which is the opposing discontinuity (disease) view; c and d are invented phrases. Key note: some severe disorders, such as psychotic states, do look qualitatively different, and DSM and ICD diagnoses are categorical; but the standard teaching is that abnormality is mainly a quantitative deviation, so b stands.", "topic": "Abnormality as a continuum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which model may explain the concept of \"Anxiety Neurosis\" in a clearer way?", "options": {"a": "Medical Model", "b": "Organic Model", "c": "Psychoanalytic Model", "d": "Learning theory model"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the psychoanalytic model explains anxiety neurosis most clearly. The term itself is psychoanalytic. Sigmund Freud (1895) separated 'anxiety neurosis' from the broad category of neurasthenia, describing general irritability, anxious expectation and anxiety attacks. His first explanation was that undischarged sexual tension turned into anxiety. In Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) he recast it: anxiety is the ego's danger signal that a forbidden id impulse is about to break through, so neurotic anxiety is fear of one's own instincts. The ego responds with repression and other defence mechanisms; when they fail, free-floating anxiety and symptoms appear. Freud distinguished realistic anxiety (from real danger), neurotic anxiety (from the id) and moral anxiety (from the superego). Example: Riya feels a constant, vague dread with no clear cause; a psychoanalyst would trace it to an unacceptable wish she has repressed and help her bring it into awareness. Option c is right because both the concept of anxiety neurosis and its classic explanation come from psychoanalysis. Exam trap: d (learning theory) is a strong rival because conditioning explains phobias well, but anxiety neurosis as a concept is Freud's, and learning theory explains specific fears more clearly than diffuse neurotic anxiety. Key note: today generalised anxiety is explained by cognitive behavioural and biological models too, but the concept of anxiety neurosis comes from psychoanalysis, so c stands.", "topic": "Anxiety neurosis: psychoanalytic model", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder is generally caused by:", "options": {"a": "Hereditary factors", "b": "Environmental factors", "c": "Psychoemotional factors", "d": "Cognitive factors"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: older texts teach that obsessive-compulsive disorder is caused mainly by psychoemotional factors. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) has two parts: obsessions, unwanted intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety (fear of dirt, of harming someone, of blasphemy), and compulsions, repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety (washing, checking, counting, praying). Sufferers usually know the thoughts are excessive but cannot stop them. In the psychoanalytic account, Sigmund Freud (1909, the Rat Man case) saw obsessional neurosis as a conflict over aggressive and sexual impulses, later traced to the anal stage, held in check by defences such as isolation, undoing and reaction formation; the rituals 'undo' the forbidden wish. In this view the cause is psychological and emotional: anxiety from inner conflict. Example: Arjun has a sudden thought of pushing his brother and then must touch the door frame seven times to feel safe; the ritual soothes the anxiety the thought created. Option c is right because the classical textbook model traces OCD to psychoemotional conflict and anxiety. Exam trap: d is tempting, since modern cognitive models stress thinking errors such as inflated responsibility (Paul Salkovskis, 1985), but the item follows the older psychogenic account. Key note: modern research shows OCD has no single cause; twin studies suggest it is moderately heritable (roughly 40 to 50 per cent), circuits linking the orbitofrontal cortex, striatum and thalamus are involved, and serotonin-acting drugs help; among these options, psychoemotional factors is the textbook answer for this old item, so c stands.", "topic": "Causes of OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "De-addiction centres are meant for:", "options": {"a": "Drug abuse treatment", "b": "Delinquency control", "c": "Panic disorders", "d": "PTSD Management"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: de-addiction centres are meant for the treatment of drug abuse. A de-addiction centre treats people with substance use problems: alcohol, opioids (heroin, prescription painkillers), cannabis, tobacco, sedatives and other drugs. Treatment usually runs in stages. First comes detoxification, medically managed withdrawal, in which doctors ease symptoms such as tremors, sweating and craving. Then comes the harder work: counselling, motivational interviewing, family therapy, relapse prevention (G. Alan Marlatt and Judith Gordon, 1985, who taught clients to cope with high-risk situations), and rehabilitation to help the person return to work and family life. In India such centres are run by government hospitals under the Health Ministry's Drug De-addiction Programme, by the National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre of AIIMS, by rehabilitation centres for addicts supported by the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, and by NGOs. Example: Kabir, dependent on alcohol for five years, spends ten days in a de-addiction ward for detoxification and then attends weekly counselling and a self-help group to stay sober. Option a is right because the purpose of these centres is the treatment of substance abuse and dependence. Exam trap: addiction overlaps with crime, panic and trauma, so options about delinquency or PTSD sound plausible; but a de-addiction centre is defined by its target, substance use, not by the problems that may accompany it.", "topic": "De-addiction centres", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Human behaviour cannot be subjected to experimentation primarily because of:", "options": {"a": "Extreme variation in experimental findings", "b": "Predominance of subjectivity", "c": "Conceptual vagueness", "d": "Limited operationalization of behaviour"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the main reason given for why human behaviour resists experimentation is the predominance of subjectivity. An experiment needs variables that can be observed, measured and controlled. Much of human behaviour, though, depends on inner states such as thoughts, feelings, intentions, values and free choice, which are private and known directly only to the person. Subjectivity enters from three sides. First, the participant's inner life cannot be observed directly. Second, the participant reacts to being studied: in the Hawthorne studies (Western Electric, 1924 to 1932) workers' output was reported to rise whatever was changed, apparently because they knew they were being watched, and Martin Orne (1962) showed that subjects pick up demand characteristics, cues about what the experimenter wants, and play along. Third, the experimenter's own expectations colour the results (Robert Rosenthal, 1966, experimenter expectancy effects). Example: Meera, in a memory experiment, guesses the hypothesis and tries harder in one condition, so her score reflects her guess rather than the independent variable. Option b is right because subjectivity is the root problem from which the other listed difficulties follow. Exam trap: c and d (conceptual vagueness, limited operationalisation) sound technical, but they are consequences of subjectivity, not the primary reason. Key note: psychology does run controlled experiments on humans, so this is the classic objection the item asks about, not a settled fact; among the options the root reason given in texts is subjectivity, so b stands.", "topic": "Limits of experimentation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following situations demonstrates a functional relationship between variables?", "options": {"a": "Y = (X)", "b": "Y = f(X)", "c": "Y = (Y·X)", "d": "Y = (X·Y)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: Y = f(X) expresses a functional relationship between variables. In mathematics a function is a rule that links each value of one variable to a value of another. Y = f(X) is read 'Y is a function of X': Y depends on X, so when X changes, Y changes in a lawful way. In an experiment X is the independent variable (IV), the factor the researcher manipulates, and Y is the dependent variable (DV), the behaviour measured; the aim of the experiment is to discover the form of f, the rule linking them. The best-known psychological example is Kurt Lewin's (1936) formula B = f(P, E): behaviour is a function of the person and the environment. Psychophysics works the same way: Gustav Fechner's (1860) law says sensation is a function of the logarithm of stimulus intensity. Example: Arjun varies the hours of sleep students get before a test (X) and measures their scores (Y); if scores rise with sleep up to a point, he has found a functional relationship, scores = f(sleep). Option b is right because only b uses the function sign f, which states that Y depends on X. Exam trap: a, Y = (X), looks almost the same but simply says Y equals X, an identity with no rule of dependence, while c and d are products in which Y appears on both sides.", "topic": "Functional relationship Y = f(X)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An experiment shall be subjected to a gross error when:", "options": {"a": "DV is under the influence of any extraneous variable", "b": "IV is further reduced to newer concepts", "c": "Sample size continues to deviate", "d": "Mood fluctuations are rampant"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: an experiment suffers a gross error when the DV is under the influence of an extraneous variable. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV), hoping to show that the IV caused any change. An extraneous variable is any other factor that could affect the DV: time of day, noise, participants' age, practice, fatigue, the experimenter's manner. If such a factor varies along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable, and the result can no longer be attributed to the IV. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the main threats to internal validity: history, maturation, testing, instrumentation, statistical regression, selection, experimental mortality and their interactions. This is a systematic (gross) error, not a small random one, because it biases the result in one direction. Example: Meera tests a new teaching method on a morning class and the old method on a tired afternoon class; the morning class scores higher, but time of day, not the method, may explain it. Controls include randomisation, elimination, holding conditions constant, matching, counterbalancing and building the variable into the design. Option a is right because uncontrolled extraneous influence on the DV destroys the causal conclusion. Exam trap: d (mood fluctuations) is one example of an extraneous variable, so it looks right, but it is only one case; a states the general rule.", "topic": "Extraneous variables and confounding", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Perceptual constancy refers to:", "options": {"a": "Seeing no change in objects repeated frequently", "b": "Seeing no change in objects despite a change in sensory information", "c": "Seeing no change in objects despite the change in their form and shape", "d": "Seeing no change in objects under any illumination"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: perceptual constancy means seeing objects as unchanged despite a change in sensory information. The image an object casts on the retina changes all the time: it shrinks as the object moves away, changes shape as the object tilts, and changes in brightness and colour as the light changes. Yet we perceive the object itself as stable. Size constancy: a friend walking away does not seem to shrink, because the brain scales the retinal image by perceived distance (Emil Emmert, 1881, showed this with afterimages). Shape constancy: a door swinging open casts a trapezoid image but still looks rectangular. Brightness and colour constancy: white paper looks white in sunlight and in shade, and a red apple looks red under yellowish lamplight. Constancy depends on cues such as distance, context and familiarity; when the cues are removed or falsified, as in the Ames room of Adelbert Ames Jr., it breaks down. Example: Kabir sees his mother waving from the far end of a long railway platform; her retinal image is tiny, but he perceives her as her normal size. Option b is right because constancy is defined by stable perception against changing sensory input. Exam trap: c sounds similar but says the object's form and shape change; in constancy the object does not change, only its image on the retina does, and c also covers only shape.", "topic": "Perceptual constancy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one is not considered as a law of perceptual organization?", "options": {"a": "Contiguity", "b": "Proximity", "c": "Specificity", "d": "Similarity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: specificity is not a law of perceptual organisation. Gestalt psychologists, led by Max Wertheimer (1923, 'Laws of organisation in perceptual forms'), showed that we do not see scattered parts but organised wholes: the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The brain groups elements by a set of laws. Proximity (nearness): items close together are seen as a group. Many Indian texts add contiguity, nearness in space or time, as a closely related factor, so events that occur together are seen as belonging together. Similarity: alike items (same colour, shape or size) group. Closure: gaps are filled so that complete figures are seen. Continuity (good continuation): elements on a smooth line or curve group. Common fate: items moving together group. Figure-ground: one part stands out as a figure against a background. Above all stands Prägnanz: we see the simplest, most stable organisation the conditions allow. Example: Riya looks at a row of dots spaced in pairs and sees four pairs rather than eight separate dots; that is proximity. Option c is right because specificity names no grouping principle; the word belongs to other areas, such as the doctrine of specific nerve energies of Johannes Muller (Handbook of Human Physiology, 1833 to 1840) or Charles Spearman's (1904) specific factors. Exam trap: contiguity sounds like a learning term (Edwin Guthrie's contiguity theory, 1935), so a looks like the odd one out; but in perception texts contiguity is listed as a grouping factor alongside proximity. Key note: Max Wertheimer's own list does not use the word contiguity, so a purist might pick a; but contiguity is nearness in space or time, a form of proximity, while specificity names no grouping principle at all, so c stands.", "topic": "Gestalt laws of organisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Reaction time is defined as:", "options": {"a": "Time between onset of stimulus and beginning of response", "b": "Time elapsing between onset of a stimulus and beginning of a response", "c": "Responding time of an individual", "d": "Fastness of a response toward stimulus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: reaction time is the time elapsing between the onset of a stimulus and the beginning of a response. Reaction time (RT) is one of the oldest measures in experimental psychology. Franciscus Donders (1868) used it to time mental processes by subtraction. In simple RT there is one stimulus and one response (press a key when a light appears); in go/no-go RT the person responds to one stimulus but not to another; in choice RT different stimuli need different responses. Subtracting one RT from another timed the added mental stage: go/no-go minus simple RT gave the time to identify the stimulus, and choice minus go/no-go the time to choose the response. RT is measured from the instant the stimulus appears to the instant the response begins, not to when it ends; for a simple reaction to a light it is roughly a fifth of a second, and a little faster to sound. RT lengthens with more choices (Hick's law, William Hick, 1952), with fatigue, alcohol and age, and shortens with practice, alertness and stronger stimuli. Example: in a laboratory practical Arjun presses a key as soon as a buzzer sounds; the chronoscope records 0.17 seconds between the buzzer's onset and his finger starting to move. Option b is right because it gives the full textbook definition, fixing both ends of the interval: onset of the stimulus and beginning of the response. Exam trap: c and d describe speed of responding in general, not a measured interval between two defined points. Key note: a says the same thing in fewer words, so both a and b are accurate; b is the textbook wording, so b stands.", "topic": "Reaction time", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Span of attention is susceptible to wider fluctuations:", "options": {"a": "During childhood", "b": "Adolescence", "c": "Adulthood", "d": "Old age"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the span of attention shows its widest fluctuations during childhood. Span of attention has two senses in textbooks. One is the number of items a person can grasp in a single brief glance: William Stanley Jevons (1871) found he never erred with three or four beans, and tachistoscope studies put the adult span at about six to eight unrelated items. The other is how long attention can stay fixed on one task. Both are small and unstable in young children. A child's attention is captured by every new sound, colour or movement (external determinants dominate), the frontal lobes that support voluntary, sustained attention are still maturing, and interests change quickly. So the child's attention jumps from object to object and lasts only a short time. With age, voluntary control grows, interests become stable, and the span widens and steadies. Adulthood shows the most stable span; old age brings some narrowing of divided and sustained attention, but not the wide swings of childhood. Example: five-year-old Meera starts a jigsaw, drops it to chase a cat, returns, then wanders off to the television, all within ten minutes; her teacher plans short, varied activities for this reason. Option a is right because instability of attention is greatest in childhood. Exam trap: fluctuation of attention in the narrow sense (attention to a faint, unchanging stimulus waxes and wanes every few seconds) occurs at every age; the item asks about developmental instability, which is widest in childhood. Key note: the stem loosely joins span and fluctuation, and attention also grows less steady in old age; but the child's attention is the most easily captured and shifted, so a stands.", "topic": "Span of attention and age", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which score on the following variables can be treated as continuous?", "options": {"a": "Scores on intelligence test", "b": "Scores on number of trees in a forest", "c": "No. of psychology books on bookshelf", "d": "No. of hair on one's head"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: scores on an intelligence test can be treated as continuous. A continuous variable can, in principle, take any value within a range, including fractions: height, weight, time, temperature, and psychological traits such as intelligence, anxiety and aptitude. Between any two values there is always another possible value. A discrete variable takes only separate, whole values, usually counts: number of children, books, trees or hairs. Nobody has 3.5 trees. Intelligence is assumed to be a continuous trait spread smoothly across people, so test scores are treated as continuous even though they are reported in whole numbers. Henry Garrett (1926, Statistics in Psychology and Education) explains that each score stands for an interval: a score of 45 covers 44.5 to 45.5, its exact limits. This is why class intervals, histograms and the normal curve can be applied to test scores. Example: Kabir scores 108 on an intelligence test and his friend scores 109; we can imagine an ability level of 108.4 between them, even if the test cannot show it, but Kabir cannot own 108.4 psychology books. Option a is right because intelligence is a continuous trait measured on a scale with no gaps in principle. Exam trap: d (hairs on a head) involves huge numbers and feels continuous, but size does not matter; anything counted in whole units is discrete.", "topic": "Continuous and discrete variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An Ogive represents:", "options": {"a": "Rank ordered distributions", "b": "Co-related distributions", "c": "Percentile distributions", "d": "Sociometric distributions"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: an ogive represents a percentile (cumulative) distribution. An ogive is a cumulative frequency curve. To draw it, you add up the frequencies class by class from the bottom (cumulative frequency, cf), usually convert them to cumulative percentages, and plot each against the upper exact limit of its class interval, then join the points with a smooth line. The result rises in an S shape from 0 to 100 per cent. Francis Galton (1875) borrowed the architects' word 'ogive' for this curve. Its main use is to read percentiles and percentile ranks directly: draw a horizontal line from any percentage to the curve and then down to the score axis. The median is the 50th percentile, the first quartile (Q1) the 25th and the third quartile (Q3) the 75th. An ogive also lets two groups be compared across the whole range of scores. Example: from her class ogive, Riya finds that a score of 62 lies at the 80th percentile, so 80 per cent of her classmates scored below 62. Option c is right because the ogive's purpose is to show cumulative percentage, that is, percentile, distributions. Exam trap: a (rank ordered) is tempting because percentiles are a kind of rank, but the ogive plots cumulative frequencies of grouped scores, not a list of ranks; b and d belong to the scatter diagram and the sociogram.", "topic": "Ogive and percentiles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one is not a measure of dispersion?", "options": {"a": "The range", "b": "The variance", "c": "Error-Variable", "d": "The standard deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: error variable is not a measure of dispersion. A measure of dispersion (variability) tells how spread out scores are around the centre. Two classes can have the same mean of 50, yet in one most scores lie between 45 and 55 while in the other they range from 10 to 90; the average hides this difference, and dispersion reveals it. The standard measures are the range (highest minus lowest score, quick but based on only two scores), the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range ((Q3 minus Q1) / 2, used with the median), the mean deviation (the average of absolute deviations from the mean), the variance (the mean of squared deviations from the mean) and the standard deviation (the square root of the variance, the most stable and widely used, named by Karl Pearson in 1893). The coefficient of variation (SD divided by the mean, times 100) compares relative spread. Example: Arjun's test scores have an SD of 4 and Meera's an SD of 12; Meera's performance is far less consistent, even though both average 60. Option c is right because 'error variable' is not a descriptive statistic of spread at all. Exam trap: the phrase echoes 'error variance', which is real, but error variance is a component of total variance in analysis of variance and in reliability theory (total variance = true variance plus error variance), not a measure of the spread of a set of scores.", "topic": "Measures of dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Gifted underachievers can be subjected to pre and post intervention treatment in a:", "options": {"a": "Face to face contact programme", "b": "Indepth interview programme", "c": "Counselling programme", "d": "Guidance Programme"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: gifted underachievers are best given pre and post intervention treatment in a counselling programme. A gifted underachiever is a student of high ability (high IQ or aptitude) whose school achievement falls well below that ability. Common causes are low motivation, poor study habits, boredom with an unchallenging curriculum, perfectionism and fear of failure, family conflict and emotional problems. Because the causes are personal and emotional, the remedy is a planned counselling programme: individual and group sessions, study skills and goal setting, motivation building, and work with parents and teachers. Such a programme is a treatment, so it fits a one-group pretest-posttest design (Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, 1963): measure achievement, motivation or study habits before the programme, run it, then measure again and compare. For example, Kabir scores 130 on an IQ test but keeps failing mathematics; after twelve weeks of counselling, his study habits score and marks are compared with his baseline. Option c is right because counselling is the structured helping intervention that works on the causes of underachievement and whose results can be measured before and after. Exam trap: guidance and counselling are paired so often that students treat them as one thing. Guidance is the broader, mainly informational service (information, tests and advice for choices); counselling is the intensive, face-to-face helping relationship that works on a problem. Key note: a school guidance programme may include such counselling, so d is tempting; but a treatment with pre and post measures is a counselling intervention, so c stands.", "topic": "Counselling gifted underachievers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Most important element of a counseling process is:", "options": {"a": "Structuring", "b": "Communication", "c": "Rapport building", "d": "Secrecy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: rapport building is the most important element of the counselling process. Rapport is a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client in which the client feels safe, understood and respected. It is built from the first contact: greeting the client warmly, explaining the purpose of the sessions and the limits of confidentiality, starting with neutral topics, listening attentively and accepting the client without judging. Every later stage depends on it. The counselling process usually runs from relationship building through assessment, goal setting and intervention to termination, and without rapport the client will not disclose real problems, accept feedback or follow a plan. Research on the therapeutic alliance points the same way: Edward Bordin (1979) described it as agreement on goals, agreement on tasks and an emotional bond, and the quality of this relationship is one of the most consistent predictors of outcome across therapies. For example, Meera comes to the college counsellor saying only that she 'cannot study'; only in the second session, once she trusts the counsellor, does she mention that her parents are separating. Option c is right because rapport is the foundation on which every other part of counselling rests. Exam trap: structuring sounds technical and important, but it is one early step (explaining the nature, roles, limits and goals of counselling), and it works only once some rapport exists.", "topic": "Rapport in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In absence of family counseling which measure is generally adopted in resolution of family conflicts?", "options": {"a": "Addressing them to family head", "b": "Fighting among the concerned members", "c": "Going to the Court of law", "d": "Compromising on primary issues"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: when there is no family counselling, family conflicts are generally resolved by addressing them to the head of the family. In the traditional Indian family, especially the joint family in which several generations share one household, the head of the family (often the eldest male, called the karta in a Hindu undivided family) or the senior elders act as mediators. Each side presents its case, the head listens, weighs the family's interests and customs, and gives a decision that members are expected to accept out of respect for age and authority. This informal mediation keeps disputes inside the family, protects its reputation and avoids the cost and publicity of outside action. For example, when Arjun and his younger brother Rohan quarrel over who will run the family shop, they take the matter to their grandfather, who divides the work and settles it. Indian clinicians see the same pattern in therapy: families usually accompany patients and share decisions, and Jaswant Singh Neki (1973) proposed the wise, guiding guru-chela relationship as an Indian therapeutic model. Option a is right because referral to the family head is the customary, generally adopted mechanism when professional help is absent. Exam trap: 'compromise' sounds like the mature answer, but giving way on primary (core) issues usually leaves resentment, and in practice even a compromise is usually brokered by the family head. Key note: this item reflects the Indian joint family context, and mediation by elders overlaps with compromise; but referring the matter to the family head is the generally adopted measure, so a stands.", "topic": "Family conflict in Indian families", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Most important characteristic of a counselor is:", "options": {"a": "His ability to listen properly", "b": "His empathetic attitude", "c": "His capability to maintain confidentiality", "d": "His professional skills"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the most important characteristic of a counsellor is an empathic attitude. Empathy is the ability to understand the client's feelings and meanings from the client's own frame of reference, as if one were in the client's place without losing the 'as if' quality, and to communicate that understanding back. Carl Rogers (1957), in 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', listed six conditions, three of which (the core conditions) the counsellor must offer: accurate empathic understanding, unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance without conditions) and congruence (genuineness). Counselling texts single out empathy as the counsellor quality most closely tied to the client feeling understood, and process research links it consistently with good outcomes. Robert Carkhuff (1969) built a five-level empathy scale for training helpers, and Gerard Egan (1975) separated primary empathy from advanced empathy. For example, when Riya says 'Nobody at home even notices me', an empathic counsellor replies 'You feel invisible and hurt in your own family', instead of giving advice or defending her parents. Option b is right because empathy is the attitude that makes the client feel understood, and every technique works through it. Exam trap: empathy is not sympathy (feeling sorry for the client), and 'professional skills' looks like the sensible answer, but Rogers argued that techniques without the core attitudes do not produce change. Key note: active listening is a close rival, since empathy is shown through listening, and Rogers later called congruence the most basic condition (not an option here); but listening is the skill and empathy the attitude it expresses, so b stands.", "topic": "Empathy as core counsellor quality", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "For resolution of emotional problems which type of counseling can be recommended for better results?", "options": {"a": "Appraisal Counseling", "b": "Vocational Counseling", "c": "Personal Counseling", "d": "Rehabilitation Counseling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: for emotional problems, personal counselling is the type that gives better results. Indian guidance texts divide guidance and counselling into three main areas: educational (choice of courses, study problems), vocational (choosing and adjusting to a career) and personal (emotional, social and adjustment problems). Personal counselling deals with the client's feelings and relationships: anxiety, low self-esteem, loneliness, grief, conflict with parents or friends, adjustment to a new college or the end of a relationship. It aims at emotional relief, self-understanding and better adjustment, using rapport, empathic listening, clarification of feelings and problem solving. It is usually individual and confidential, though group formats exist. For example, Kabir has moved from his village to a city hostel; he feels homesick, cries at night and cannot concentrate. His difficulty is emotional, not a lack of career information or a disability, so the counsellor offers personal counselling. Option c is right because personal counselling is the type designed specifically for emotional and adjustment problems. Exam trap: rehabilitation counselling also meets distressed clients, so it tempts; but it is defined by its goal of restoring independent functioning after disability, illness or addiction, not by emotional problems in general.", "topic": "Personal counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An underachiever is one who achieves:", "options": {"a": "Less than his IQ", "b": "More than his IQ", "c": "Less than his AQ", "d": "More than his AQ"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: an underachiever is one who achieves less than his IQ (measured ability) predicts. An intelligence test estimates how much a student should be capable of learning; an achievement test measures what the student has actually learnt. When the two match, the student is working to capacity. When achievement falls clearly below the level expected from IQ or aptitude, the student is an underachiever; when achievement is higher than the IQ predicts, usually through hard work and strong motivation, the student is an overachiever. Older texts turned this into a ratio. The educational quotient is EQ = educational age / chronological age x 100, and dividing it by the IQ gives the achievement (or accomplishment) quotient, AQ = EQ / IQ x 100, which equals educational age / mental age x 100; the idea was worked out in the 1920s, for example in Raymond Franzen's The Accomplishment Ratio (1922). An AQ below 100 means achievement lags behind ability. Today the same idea appears as the ability-achievement discrepancy. For example, Arjun has an IQ of 125, which predicts top marks, but his achievement scores are only average; he is an underachiever, and the counsellor looks for causes such as poor study habits, low motivation or stress at home. Option a is right because underachievement is defined as achievement falling short of measured ability. Exam trap: AQ already is the achievement-to-ability ratio, so 'less than his AQ' or 'more than his AQ' compares achievement with itself and makes no sense; the comparison must be with IQ.", "topic": "Underachievement and the AQ", "fig": ""}, {"q": "CBT targets:", "options": {"a": "The client", "b": "The behaviour", "c": "The perception", "d": "The emotion"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) targets the perception, that is, the way the client interprets and appraises events. Its central principle, going back to the Stoic philosopher Epictetus (about 50 to 135 CE), is that people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. Aaron Beck noticed around 1960 that depressed patients had quick negative automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions (in his 1960s papers arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation; personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking were added in 1976) rooted in deeper core beliefs, and set out the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). Albert Ellis (1955), founder of what is now called rational emotive behaviour therapy, put the same idea as the ABC model: an Activating event leads to emotional Consequences through the person's Beliefs about it. Change the perception and the emotion and behaviour change with it. In practice, therapist and client identify thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and build more realistic alternatives. For example, Riya fails one test and thinks 'I am stupid and will never pass'; CBT helps her weigh the evidence (she passed the last four tests) and reframe it as 'I did not prepare this topic well', which eases her hopelessness. Option c is right because faulty perception and interpretation of events is the defining target of CBT. Exam trap: the word 'behaviour' in the name pulls students to b, but behaviour change in CBT is largely a means of testing and changing thoughts. Key note: CBT works on both thoughts and behaviour (behavioural experiments, activity scheduling), so b is close; but its defining target is the faulty perception or interpretation, so c stands.", "topic": "CBT and cognitive appraisal", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "Humanistic counseling is generally attributed to:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Carl Rogers", "c": "William Thompson", "d": "Joseph Breuer"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: humanistic counselling is generally attributed to Carl Rogers. Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, introduced non-directive counselling, and in Client-Centered Therapy (1951) he developed it into client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, the main humanistic approach to counselling. Its assumptions are humanistic: every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive towards growth; problems arise from incongruence between the self-concept and actual experience, created by conditions of worth; and the client, not the counsellor, is the expert on his or her own life. So the counsellor does not diagnose, interpret or advise, but offers three core conditions: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). Given these, the client's own growth forces take over. Abraham Maslow (Motivation and Personality, 1954) is the other founder of humanistic psychology, but as a theorist of motivation rather than of counselling. For example, when Kabir cannot decide between engineering and music, a Rogerian counsellor reflects his feelings ('You feel torn between what your family expects and what you love') and trusts him to reach his own decision. Option b is right because Rogers created the humanistic, client-centred approach to counselling. Exam trap: the 'talking cure' of Josef Breuer's patient Anna O. sounds like counselling, and Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis on it, but both names belong to psychoanalysis; humanistic psychology arose as the 'third force', against both psychoanalysis and behaviourism.", "topic": "Rogers and humanistic counselling", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Vocational guidance emphasizes:", "options": {"a": "Personal appraisal", "b": "Personal strengths and weakness analysis", "c": "Fitting appraisal data to the market requirement", "d": "Providing occupational information to the needy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: vocational guidance emphasises fitting the individual's appraisal data to the requirements of the job market. Frank Parsons (Choosing a Vocation, 1909), the father of vocational guidance, set out three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests, ambitions and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions, pay and prospects of different lines of work; and (3) 'true reasoning' on the relations between these two groups of facts. The third step, matching the person to the job, is the heart of the process. Edmund G. Williamson (How to Counsel Students, 1939) developed this into the trait-and-factor approach: measure the person's traits with aptitude tests, interest inventories and school records, describe the factors each job demands, and match the two. Later career theories kept the same idea of person-job fit. For example, Meera's tests show high clerical speed, strong numerical ability and orderly, conventional interests; the counsellor matches this profile to openings in banking and accounts rather than simply reporting her scores. Option c is right because vocational guidance exists to match appraised abilities and interests to occupational demands. Exam trap: options a, b and d are each one ingredient of Parsons's process; the stem asks for the emphasis, which is the step that joins them. Key note: d (providing occupational information) is part of vocational guidance (Parsons's step 2), but the emphasis is on matching the person to the job, so c stands.", "topic": "Parsons and vocational guidance", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing:", "options": {"a": "Personality", "b": "Intelligence", "c": "Attitude", "d": "Aptitude"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing intelligence. C. M. Bhatia (1955) built the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence for Indian conditions, including people who cannot read. It has five performance subtests: Kohs block design (copying coloured designs with cubes), Alexander's pass-along test (sliding blocks in a tray into a target pattern), pattern drawing, immediate memory for digits, and picture construction (assembling cut pieces of pictures). Because it needs no reading and little language, and was standardised with separate norms for literate and illiterate children aged 11 to 16, it is used with illiterate and less schooled people and minimises the educational bias of verbal tests. The subtest scores are combined into one Performance Quotient (PQ), Bhatia's own name for it, as distinct from an IQ. It stands beside other Indian intelligence tests such as the Binet-Kamat Test (V. V. Kamat, 1930s) and Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (Arthur Malin, 1969). For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun, a 12-year-old from a village with little schooling, chooses Bhatia's Battery over the WISC, which leans on schooling and foreign norms. Option b is right because the battery is a non-verbal performance test of general intelligence. Exam trap: because its subtests look like practical, manipulative tasks, students mistake it for an aptitude test, but it yields one intelligence score, not a profile of specific aptitudes.", "topic": "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests", "fig": "C. M. Bhatia"}, {"q": "Measurement by definition is a process of:", "options": {"a": "Assigning scores to some qualitative data", "b": "Describing informative collections in numericals", "c": "Extending statistical treatments to research information", "d": "Planning transfer of qualities into quantities"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: by definition, measurement is a process of assigning scores (numbers) to qualities or attributes. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in 'On the theory of scales of measurement', defined measurement as the assignment of numerals to objects or events according to rules. In psychology the things measured are attributes of people: intelligence, anxiety, attitude, reaction time. A test or rating scale supplies the rule, so that a quality we cannot see directly becomes a quantity we can compare, such as an IQ of 110 or an anxiety score of 32. Stevens also showed that the rule fixes the level of measurement: nominal (numbers as labels), ordinal (rank order), interval (equal units, arbitrary zero) and ratio (equal units, true zero), and each level allows different statistics. Measurement is kept apart from evaluation, which judges a score against a standard (42 is a pass), and from assessment, which combines many sources to answer a question about a person. For example, when Riya's teacher rates her classroom confidence from 1 to 5, she is assigning a number to a qualitative attribute by a rule: that is measurement. Option a is right because it matches the textbook definition of measurement as assigning numbers to attributes. Exam trap: b (describing data in numbers) and c (applying statistics) describe what happens after measurement, not measurement itself. Key note: the options are loosely worded, and b and d also hint at quantifying; a is the one that matches the textbook definition of assigning numbers to attributes, so a stands.", "topic": "Definition of measurement", "fig": "Stanley Smith Stevens"}, {"q": "For effective sampling an important condition is:", "options": {"a": "Existence of a group", "b": "Sampling procedure", "c": "Research focus", "d": "Structure of group"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: an important condition for effective sampling is knowing the structure of the group (the population) to be sampled. A sample is useful only if it represents its population, and a representative sample cannot be drawn without knowing what the population is made of: its size, its boundaries (who belongs), how homogeneous or heterogeneous it is, and which subgroups (strata or clusters) it contains. This knowledge drives every later decision. A homogeneous population can be represented by a small simple random sample; a heterogeneous one needs a larger sample, and if it has clear subgroups, stratified random sampling draws from each subgroup in proportion to its size. Natural groupings such as schools or villages allow cluster sampling. Research texts list the essentials of a good sample as representativeness, adequate size, independence of selection and freedom from bias, and all of these depend on the population's structure. For example, Meera wants to survey stress in a university that is 60% women, has arts, science and commerce faculties, and has both day scholars and hostellers; knowing this structure, she stratifies by gender and faculty so that every group is fairly represented. Option d is right because the structure of the group decides the sample size, the sampling method and whether the sample can stand for the whole. Exam trap: 'existence of a group' sounds basic and therefore important, but every sample presupposes a population; mere existence says nothing about how to sample it. Key note: a sound sampling procedure (random selection) also matters, but the procedure is chosen to fit the structure of the group, so the structure is the prior condition, and d stands.", "topic": "Population structure and sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Chi-square analysis is relevant for:", "options": {"a": "Parametric data", "b": "Nonparametric data", "c": "One shot survey data", "d": "Post facto observational data"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: chi-square analysis is relevant for nonparametric data, that is, frequency counts in categories. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test (χ²) to compare the observed frequency (O) in each category with the frequency expected (E) under the null hypothesis: χ² = Σ (O - E)² / E. It is used in two main ways: the goodness-of-fit test (do the counts on one variable match an expected pattern?) and the test of independence in a contingency table (are two categorical variables related?). Chi-square is nonparametric, or distribution-free: it assumes no normal population and works with nominal (or ordinal) data, where there are no means or standard deviations, only counts. It needs raw frequencies (not percentages), each person in only one cell, and expected frequencies of about 5 or more per cell. Parametric tests (t, F, Pearson r), by contrast, assume interval or ratio scores from normal populations with similar variances. For example, Kabir asks 100 students whether they prefer online or classroom teaching and notes each student's gender; a 2 x 2 chi-square tells him whether preference is related to gender. Option b is right because chi-square is a nonparametric test for categorical frequency data. Exam trap: chi-square can be run on survey or ex post facto data, so c and d look possible, but those words name research designs, not kinds of data; what makes chi-square relevant is that the data are frequencies, whatever the design.", "topic": "Chi-square and nonparametric data", "fig": "Karl Pearson"}, {"q": "Reliability of a test is contingent upon:", "options": {"a": "Nature of the test", "b": "Length of the test", "c": "Objectivity of the test", "d": "Retesting time"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the reliability of a test is contingent upon its length. Reliability is the consistency of scores: a reliable test gives nearly the same result when a person is retested or given an equivalent form. Every observed score is a true score plus random error. Each item samples the trait with some error; when more parallel items are added, the random errors tend to cancel out while the true score accumulates, so a longer test is more reliable. The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula, published separately by Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910), predicts the effect: r new = n r / (1 + (n - 1) r), where n is the number of times the test is lengthened. If Ishaan's 20-item anxiety scale has a reliability of 0.60, doubling it to 40 comparable items gives 2(0.60) / (1 + 0.60) = 0.75. The same formula steps a split-half correlation up to full-test length. Gains shrink as the test grows, and the added items must measure the same trait. Other influences are group heterogeneity (a wide range raises r), item difficulty and discrimination, guessing, objectivity of scoring, speededness and the retest interval. Option b is right because test length is the classic determinant of reliability, with its own formula. Exam trap: objectivity concerns scorer agreement, only one source of error. Key note: objectivity of scoring and the retest interval also affect reliability, but test length is the factor the textbooks put first, with its own formula, so b stands.", "topic": "Test length and reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Construct validity of an instrument is appropriate for the situations where:", "options": {"a": "An access to concepts is desired", "b": "Concepts are reduced to numericals", "c": "Test formats are only content based", "d": "No other form of validity is applicable"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: construct validity is appropriate where no other form of validity is applicable, that is, where there is no adequate criterion or content domain. A construct is a theoretical, unobservable attribute such as anxiety, intelligence, creativity or ego strength. Content validity works when the domain can be listed and sampled (a spelling test of class 5 words); criterion-related validity works when there is an outside measure to predict (job ratings, exam marks). For many psychological traits there is neither: no single agreed criterion of 'anxiety' exists. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), in 'Construct validity in psychological tests', argued that construct validity must be investigated whenever no criterion or universe of content is accepted as entirely adequate to define the quality being measured. The test is then judged by whether its scores behave as the theory (the nomological network) predicts: convergent and discriminant correlations (Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959, the multitrait-multimethod matrix), factor analysis, group differences and change after an experimental intervention. For example, Riya builds a resilience scale; with no criterion of 'true resilience', she shows that it correlates highly with an existing resilience measure, weakly with intelligence, and rises after resilience training. Option d is right because construct validity is the approach used when criterion and content validity cannot settle the question. Exam trap: b (concepts reduced to numbers) is just measurement, and c (content-based formats) points to content validity. Key note: a ('access to concepts') also points at constructs, since a construct is a theoretical concept; but the textbook condition for using construct validity, from Cronbach and Meehl, is the lack of an adequate criterion or content domain, so d stands.", "topic": "When construct validity applies", "fig": "Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl"}, {"q": "Item analysis of a psychological scale helps in:", "options": {"a": "Deciding the length of a test", "b": "Elimination of the weaker items", "c": "Predicting the validity of a test", "d": "Comparing test results with the contemporary instruments in the field"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: item analysis helps in eliminating the weaker items of a psychological scale. After a pool of items is written and tried out on a sample, item analysis examines each item statistically to decide which to keep, revise or discard. The difficulty index (p) is the proportion of examinees who pass or endorse the item; items that almost everyone passes or fails tell no one apart, so most tests keep p between about 0.30 and 0.70. The discrimination index (D) compares upper and lower scorers on the total test, usually the top and bottom 27 percent, the split Truman Kelley (1939) showed works best; D = p upper - p lower. Items with low or negative D, or low item-total correlations, fail to separate high from low scorers, and a negative D usually means a miskeyed or ambiguous item. Distractor analysis checks that each wrong option attracts some low scorers. Weak items are then revised or dropped, and keeping only the good ones raises the reliability and validity of the final form. For example, Kabir tries out 60 items for an exam anxiety scale on 200 students; 12 items have D below 0.20 and two have negative D, so he discards them and keeps 46. Option b is right because the direct purpose of item analysis is to find and remove or revise weak items. Exam trap: c is tempting because better items do improve validity, but item analysis does not predict a validity coefficient; validity must be established separately against a criterion or a construct.", "topic": "Purpose of item analysis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which is the major difficulty in personality assessment?", "options": {"a": "Patterned individuality", "b": "Extreme subjectivity", "c": "Dynamic status", "d": "Behavioural variability"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the major difficulty in personality assessment is the dynamic status of personality. Gordon Allport (1937) defined personality as 'the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment'. Dynamic means personality is not a fixed object: it keeps growing and changing with age and experience, it is an active interplay of motives, emotions, habits and beliefs, and it shows itself differently as the person adjusts to different situations. Any test or interview therefore captures only a snapshot of a moving, organised whole, and the same person may look different next year or in another setting; because personality is also complex and unique, no single instrument captures all of it. Other difficulties follow: inventories invite faking and socially desirable answers, projective tests depend on subjective interpretation, and Walter Mischel (1968, Personality and Assessment) found that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly, with correlations rarely much above 0.30. For example, Meera scores as shy on an inventory in her first college month, yet a year later she leads the debating society. Option c is right because the dynamic, changing nature of personality is the root problem behind the other difficulties. Exam trap: subjectivity is a weakness of particular methods, not of personality itself. Key note: subjectivity and behavioural variability are real difficulties too (Mischel's critique is the famous one); the item treats the dynamic, ever-changing status of personality as the root problem, so c stands.", "topic": "Difficulties of personality assessment", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Structured testing situations are itemized in:", "options": {"a": "TAT", "b": "RIT", "c": "EPI", "d": "CAT"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: structured testing situations are itemised in the EPI. A structured test presents clear, specific items with fixed response options and objective scoring, so answers can be counted against a key and compared with norms. The Eysenck Personality Inventory (EPI), published by Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1964), has 57 yes/no questions about sociability, impulsiveness, moods and worries. They are scored for extraversion (E), neuroticism (N) and a Lie scale (L) that catches faking good. It followed the Maudsley Personality Inventory (1959) and was later replaced by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975), which added psychoticism. The other three options are projective techniques, which are deliberately unstructured: an ambiguous stimulus, minimal instructions and free responses, on the projective hypothesis that the person projects needs and conflicts into whatever structure he or she imposes on the material. For example, Arjun answers the 57 yes/no items of the EPI and receives objective E and N scores in one short sitting, whereas on the TAT he tells free stories that a trained examiner must interpret. Option c is right because the EPI is an objective, itemised inventory with fixed questions and fixed answers. Exam trap: RIT is not a familiar abbreviation; it is the Rorschach Inkblot Test, the most unstructured test of all, so do not guess that an unfamiliar set of initials must be an inventory.", "topic": "Structured versus projective tests", "fig": "Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck"}, {"q": "Aptitude by definition is nearer to:", "options": {"a": "Thurstone's PMA concept", "b": "Spearman's two factor concept", "c": "Meier's Art Judgment Test", "d": "Tandon's GMAT"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: by definition, aptitude is nearest to Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) concept of primary mental abilities (PMA). Walter Bingham (1937, Aptitudes and Aptitude Testing) defined an aptitude as a condition or set of characteristics regarded as symptomatic of a person's ability to acquire, with training, some specific knowledge, skill or set of responses. Two ideas matter: aptitudes are specific (separate abilities such as mechanical, clerical, musical or numerical), and they are potentials that predict future learning. Thurstone, using multiple factor analysis, rejected a single dominant general factor and found seven fairly independent primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. This idea of several separate abilities is the basis of the multiple aptitude batteries, such as the Differential Aptitude Tests (George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman, 1947) and the General Aptitude Test Battery (1947), which give a profile of separate scores rather than one IQ. For example, Kabir's profile shows high space and mechanical reasoning scores but average verbal ability; a counsellor reads this as an aptitude for engineering drawing rather than law. Option a is right because aptitude, like Thurstone's PMAs, means specific, separable abilities. Exam trap: Charles Spearman's (1904) specific factors (s) sound like aptitudes, but they are narrow, task-bound factors, and his theory stresses g, general intelligence. Key note: Meier's test is an aptitude test, so c is tempting; but the stem asks which concept the definition of aptitude is nearest to, and that is the PMA idea of specific abilities, so a stands.", "topic": "Aptitude and Thurstone's PMAs", "fig": "Louis Leon Thurstone"}, {"q": "Which item can best fit in a clerical aptitude test?", "options": {"a": "Time discrimination", "b": "Total memory", "c": "Alphabetical filing", "d": "Patch discrimination"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: alphabetical filing is the item that best fits a clerical aptitude test. Clerical aptitude is the potential to learn office work quickly and accurately, and its tests stress perceptual speed and accuracy with names and numbers. The classic is the Minnesota Clerical Test of Dorothy Andrew, Donald Paterson and Howard Longstaff (1933): 200 pairs of numbers and 200 pairs of names, and the examinee marks, at speed, the pairs that are exactly the same; the score is right answers minus wrong ones. Clerical batteries add alphabetising and filing (putting names into their correct place in an alphabetical list), coding, copying, checking spelling and simple arithmetic, and the Differential Aptitude Tests include a clerical speed and accuracy subtest. These tasks are small samples of real office work, which is why they predict success in it; in terms of Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) primary mental abilities they draw chiefly on perceptual speed. For example, Riya is asked to insert 'Mehta, R.' and 19 other names into an alphabetical list as fast as she can; her speed and accuracy show how quickly she would learn to file records in an office. Option c is right because alphabetical filing is a direct sample of clerical work, testing speed and accuracy with verbal material. Exam trap: 'discrimination' items sound like checking tasks, but time and patch (colour) discrimination are sensory judgements used in musical or perceptual tests, not clerical checking of names and numbers.", "topic": "Clerical aptitude test items", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2010": [{"q": "Which of the following is currently outside general psychology?", "concept": "The answer is c: aviation psychology is the field that lies outside general psychology. General psychology is the core course that teaches the basic principles of behaviour and mental processes shared by everyone: sensation, perception, attention, learning, memory, motivation, emotion, intelligence and personality. Two basic fields sit inside it. Physiological psychology studies the biological bases of behaviour (the nervous system, glands and hormones). Psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), measures how the physical intensity of a stimulus relates to the sensation it produces, and it became the foundation of experimental psychology. Applied fields, by contrast, carry these principles into one particular setting. Aviation psychology is a narrow applied speciality: selecting and training pilots, designing cockpits and instruments, and studying fatigue and human error in flight. It grew out of pilot selection in the First and Second World Wars. Example: Arjun meets thresholds and the nervous system in general psychology, but pilot selection only in a specialised aviation course. Option c is right because aviation psychology applies psychology to one occupation rather than describing basic processes common to all people. Exam trap: examiners pair a narrow applied speciality with a broad, familiar applied branch, so the rule \"any applied field is outside\" makes both c and d look right. Key note: industrial psychology is also an applied field, but it is a broad, long-established branch (Hugo Munsterberg, Psychology and Industrial Efficiency, 1913) that most general psychology courses still introduce; aviation psychology is the one that sits clearly outside, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Physiological psychology studies the biological bases of behaviour (brain, nerves, glands, hormones); it is a basic field taught inside general psychology, so it is not outside it.", "b": "Psychophysics is Gustav Fechner's (1860) study of how stimulus intensity relates to sensation, a basic field at the root of experimental psychology and a standard chapter of general psychology.", "d": "Industrial psychology (Hugo Munsterberg, 1913) is applied, but it is a broad, long-established branch that general psychology courses still introduce, so it is not the field that lies clearly outside."}}, {"q": "While a Psychologist emphasizes human behaviour, Psychology stresses:", "concept": "The answer is c: psychology stresses individuality. The stem is garbled in print, but it is the standard textbook contrast between sociology and psychology. Sociology studies human groups, society and its institutions (family, caste, religion, the state): how people behave as members of groups and how social structures shape them. A classic example is Emile Durkheim's Suicide (1897), which explained differences in suicide rates between groups by how well each group integrated its members. Psychology studies the individual: one person's behaviour and mental processes, and the individual differences in ability, temperament and personality that make each person unique. Both disciplines study human behaviour; they differ in the unit of analysis. Social psychology sits between them, asking how the individual is influenced by other people. Example: a sociologist might compare exam stress across states and income groups, while a psychologist asks why Meera, but not her classmate, panics before every test. Option c is right because individuality is the distinguishing emphasis of psychology as against sociology. Exam trap: motivation, emotion and cognitive growth are all real topics within psychology, so they look attractive; but they are subject areas inside the discipline, not the feature that sets psychology apart from another discipline. Key note: the paper prints \"While a Psychologist emphasizes human behaviour\"; it almost certainly meant \"While a sociologist emphasises group behaviour\". Read that way, c is the only option that contrasts psychology with another discipline, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Human motivation (why behaviour is started, directed and sustained) is one topic area within psychology, not the emphasis that distinguishes psychology from sociology.", "b": "Emotional behaviour is another content area of psychology (feelings and their expression); it does not mark psychology's focus on the individual as against the group.", "d": "Cognitive growth (how thinking matures through childhood) is a developmental topic inside psychology's subject matter, not its defining contrast with sociology."}}, {"q": "Besides Heredity and Environment which of the following factors is essentially concerned with growth and development?", "concept": "The answer is d: time is the third factor, besides heredity and environment, that governs growth and development. Heredity is the set of potentials passed on through the genes at conception; environment is everything that acts on the person from outside, from the womb onwards (nutrition, family, school, culture). Many texts write the relation as Development = f(Heredity x Environment x Time). Time matters for two reasons. First, growth unfolds over time through maturation, the biological ripening of the body and nervous system on a genetic timetable, so a child cannot walk before the body is ready. Second, the same experience has different effects at different ages: there are critical and sensitive periods when the organism is most open to certain influences, such as language in early childhood, or the damage a drug can do in the first weeks of pregnancy. Example: Kabir, adopted abroad at six months, speaks the new language with a native accent, while his cousin who moved at fifteen keeps a foreign one; same environment, different timing. Option d is right because time adds a separate dimension, the age and timing at which heredity and environment interact. Exam trap: \"nature\" looks like a new factor, but it is only another word for heredity (the nature and nurture phrase popularised by Francis Galton, 1874), so it repeats the stem. Key note: many texts name only heredity and environment as the two factors; among the options only time adds a genuinely separate factor, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Gender here stands for biological sex, which is fixed by the sex chromosomes at conception, so it falls under heredity rather than being a third factor.", "b": "Climate is part of the physical environment, so it is already covered by the stem's \"environment\".", "c": "Nature is a synonym for heredity (Francis Galton's nature versus nurture, 1874), so choosing it simply repeats a factor the stem already names."}}, {"q": "CR is contingent upon:", "concept": "The answer is a: the conditioned response (CR) is contingent upon the conditioned stimulus (CS). In the classical conditioning described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), food in a dog's mouth is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), which elicits salivation, the unconditioned response (UCR), without any learning. A neutral sound, such as a metronome or bell, is presented just before the food many times. After this pairing the sound alone produces salivation: the sound has become the CS, and salivation to it is the CR. The CR is defined by its link to the CS. It appears only when the CS is presented, and it weakens (extinction) if the CS keeps appearing without the UCS. Robert Rescorla (1968) sharpened the idea: conditioning depends on the contingency between CS and UCS, that is, on the CS reliably predicting the UCS, not on mere pairing in time. Example: Riya's mouth waters when she hears the pressure cooker whistle, because the whistle has always come just before lunch; the whistle is the CS and her watering mouth is the CR. Option a is right because the CR is, by definition, the learned response triggered by the CS. Exam trap: the UCS is tempting, since conditioning cannot happen without it during learning; but the UCS elicits the UCR, while the CR, whenever it occurs, depends on the CS. Key note: the CR is learned only because the CS was paired with the UCS, and it extinguishes when the UCS stops; but what the CR is contingent on when it occurs is the CS, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The UCS (food) naturally elicits the UCR without learning; it is needed to build the association, but the learned response itself is triggered by the CS.", "c": "The UCR is the unlearned response to the UCS (salivation to food); it is a response, not a stimulus, so the CR cannot be contingent on it.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a is correct: the CS is the stimulus that elicits the CR."}}, {"q": "Cognitive mapping is linked with:", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive mapping is linked with insight learning, the other classic cognitive form of learning. Edward Tolman (1948), in \"Cognitive maps in rats and men\", argued that rats learn a mental map of a maze rather than a chain of turns. In the latent learning study of Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930), rats that explored a maze unrewarded for ten days matched the always-rewarded rats within a day or two of food being introduced: they had learned the layout all along. Tolman called his view purposive behaviourism and drew on Gestalt ideas. Insight learning comes from Wolfgang Kohler (1925), whose chimpanzee Sultan, playing idly with two short sticks, fitted them together and at once used them to rake in a banana out of reach. Both treat learning as a mental reorganisation of the relations in a situation, not as stimulus-response links stamped in by reward. Example: when her usual road is closed, Meera at once takes a new lane home, because she carries a map of the area in her head; that is cognitive learning, not a drilled habit. Option a is right because cognitive maps and insight both belong to the cognitive, Gestalt-influenced view of learning. Exam trap: Tolman ran rats in mazes, the behaviourists' own apparatus, so conditioning looks right; but his point was that rats learn more than S-R habits. Key note: the textbook pair for cognitive maps is latent learning, which is not an option; among the options, insight learning is the other classic cognitive (non S-R) form of learning, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, Edward Thorndike, B. F. Skinner) explains learning as stimulus-response links strengthened by pairing or reinforcement, the very S-R view that Tolman's cognitive maps opposed.", "c": "Imitative learning is learning by copying a model (Neal Miller and John Dollard, 1941; later Albert Bandura's observational learning); it concerns models, not mental maps of the environment.", "d": "Intuitive learning is not a standard learning theory in psychology texts; it is a filler that sounds like insight."}}, {"q": "According to Ebbinghaus (1885) memory is a function of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) showed that memory is a function of time, the interval since learning. Working alone and testing himself, he learned lists of nonsense syllables (consonant-vowel-consonant trigrams such as DAX or BUP), chosen precisely because they carried no meaning, interest or earlier associations. He learned each list until he could recite it twice without error, relearned it to the same point after a set delay, and measured savings: how much less time relearning took. Plotting savings against delay gave the forgetting curve. Forgetting is fastest at first and then slows: savings fell to about 44 percent after an hour and about 34 percent after a day, then levelled off over the following month without reaching zero. Retention, in short, changes lawfully with the time elapsed. He also found that repetitions spread over several days work better than the same number massed together. Example: Arjun learns twenty new vocabulary words at 9 a.m.; most of what he will forget goes in the first hour or so, and far less over the following days. Option b is right because time since learning was the variable Ebbinghaus manipulated, and retention depended systematically on it. Exam trap: interest and motivation clearly affect everyday memory, so they look plausible; but Ebbinghaus removed them on purpose by using meaningless material, which is exactly why time stood out as the variable.", "traps": {"a": "Motivation affects how hard one tries to learn, but Ebbinghaus was his own single, steady subject and did not vary motivation; his curve relates retention to time.", "c": "Maturation is biological ripening with age, a developmental variable; Ebbinghaus was an adult testing himself over days and weeks, so maturation played no part.", "d": "Interest was deliberately excluded: nonsense syllables were chosen to remove meaning and interest, leaving time as the variable."}}, {"q": "Conflict analysis constitutes the work of:", "concept": "The answer is a: conflict analysis is the work of Kurt Lewin (1935). In his field theory, set out in A Dynamic Theory of Personality, behaviour results from forces in the person's life space: the person plus the psychological environment as he or she sees it. Goals have valence; a positive valence attracts and a negative valence repels. A conflict arises when forces of roughly equal strength pull in opposite directions. Lewin described three basic types. In approach-approach conflict there are two attractive goals and only one can be chosen; it is the easiest to resolve. In avoidance-avoidance conflict both options are unpleasant, and the person vacillates or tries to leave the field. In approach-avoidance conflict one goal both attracts and repels, so the person vacillates near the goal; it is often the hardest to settle. Neal Miller (1944) later measured approach and avoidance gradients in rats, and a fourth type, double approach-avoidance, was added. Example: Kabir wins a seat at a top college far from home; he wants the college but dreads leaving his family, a classic approach-avoidance conflict. Option a is right because the systematic analysis of conflict types in terms of valences and forces comes from Lewin's field theory. Exam trap: Wolfgang Kohler (1925) is the tempting name, because Lewin worked alongside him in the Berlin Gestalt group; but Kohler's work was insight, not conflict.", "traps": {"b": "Abraham Maslow (1943) is known for the hierarchy of needs, a theory of motivation, not for the analysis of conflict types.", "c": "Wolfgang Kohler was Lewin's Gestalt colleague in Berlin, but his work was insight learning in chimpanzees, not motivational conflict.", "d": "Edwin Guthrie (1935) proposed the contiguity theory of learning and one-trial learning, which has nothing to do with conflict analysis."}}, {"q": "For two factor theory of intelligence if factor 1 is \"S\" which is the other one?", "concept": "The answer is c: the other factor in Charles Spearman's two-factor theory is G, the general factor. Spearman (1904) invented factor analysis to explain a striking finding: schoolchildren's marks in very different subjects, and their scores on sensory tasks, all correlated positively (the positive manifold). He proposed that every intellectual task draws on two kinds of ability. The general factor, g, is common to all tasks; Spearman thought of it as a kind of mental energy, and it showed most strongly in reasoning tasks. Each task also needs its own specific factor, s, peculiar to that task alone, which is why the correlations between tests are high but never perfect. A person's score on any test therefore reflects g plus that test's s. He set the theory out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya does well in mathematics, Hindi and music; her general factor runs through all three, while her special flair for rhythm is an s factor for music. Option c is right because Spearman's theory has exactly two factors, g and s. Exam trap: the other options look like abbreviations from the later multiple-factor and hierarchical models, which added group factors between g and s; Spearman's original theory had no group factors at all.", "traps": {"a": "Wf reads like word fluency, one of Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) primary mental abilities, not a factor in Spearman's theory.", "b": "Sp looks like an abbreviation for spatial ability (space is another Thurstone primary ability), a group factor rather than Spearman's g.", "d": "Mc resembles a mechanical group factor, like the k:m (practical-mechanical) factor in Philip Vernon's (1950) hierarchy; Spearman had only g and s."}}, {"q": "Behaviourists tried to reduce emotions to:", "concept": "The answer is c: behaviourists tried to reduce emotions to behavioural frames, that is, observable patterns of bodily reaction. John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism by rejecting introspection: psychology should study only what can be observed and measured. Applied to emotion, this meant dropping inner feelings and describing emotion as a pattern of response. In Psychology from the Standpoint of a Behaviorist (1919) Watson defined an emotion as \"an hereditary 'pattern-reaction' involving profound changes of the bodily mechanism as a whole, but particularly of the visceral and glandular systems\". He held that infants show only three unlearned emotional patterns: fear (to a loud noise or sudden loss of support), rage (to hampered movement) and love (to stroking). All other emotions, he argued, are conditioned from these, as Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) tried to show by conditioning fear of a white rat in Little Albert. Example: a behaviourist would describe Arjun's fear of dogs not as a feeling but as trembling, a racing heart and running away when a dog barks. Option c is right because behaviourists described emotions purely as frames or patterns of observable behaviour. Exam trap: mentalistic concepts sound scientific, but they are exactly what behaviourists threw out. Key note: \"excitatory behaviours\" echoes the later activation (arousal) view of emotion (Elizabeth Duffy, 1941; Donald Lindsley, 1951), which is close but not the classic behaviourist account; Watson's pattern-reactions are behavioural patterns, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Mentalistic concepts (feelings, consciousness, introspective reports) are what behaviourists rejected; reducing emotion to them is the opposite of the behaviourist programme.", "b": "Sensory responses concern input, how stimuli are registered by the senses; Watson's emotions were whole-body visceral and glandular reaction patterns, not sensations.", "d": "Excitatory behaviours suggests the activation or arousal theory of Elizabeth Duffy (1941) and Donald Lindsley (1951), a later account that places emotion on one arousal dimension rather than as Watson's distinct pattern-reactions."}}, {"q": "In Maslow's scheme of motivation basal needs are shared by:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Abraham Maslow's (1943) scheme, the basal needs are shared by both humans and animals. Maslow arranged human motives in a hierarchy, set out fully in Motivation and Personality (1954): physiological needs (hunger, thirst, sleep, sex), safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation at the top. By prepotency, an unmet lower need dominates behaviour until it is largely satisfied, after which higher needs take charge. The basal (lower) needs, physiological and safety, are biological, and they are plainly present in animals too: a dog seeks food, water and shelter and avoids danger. Maslow called the basic needs instinctoid, weakly rooted in our biology. He also argued that the higher a need sits, the more distinctly human it is: we share the need for food with all living things, the need for love perhaps with the higher apes, and the need for self-actualisation with no other species. Example: when Meera has skipped lunch before an exam, her hunger, a need any animal has, pushes aside her wish to impress the examiner. Option a is right because the lower physiological and safety needs are common to humans and animals. Exam trap: because the hierarchy is a theory of human motivation, many pick \"humans only\"; but that description fits the higher needs, not the basal ones.", "traps": {"b": "Humans only fits the higher needs, especially self-actualisation, which Maslow saw as distinctly human; the basal physiological and safety needs are not unique to us.", "c": "Animals only is wrong because humans plainly have physiological and safety needs too; they form the base of the human hierarchy.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a correctly describes the basal needs as common to humans and animals."}}, {"q": "Individual space concept is core to:", "concept": "The answer is d: the concept of individual (personal) space is core to the observation method. Personal space is the invisible, portable zone around a person's body that others are not supposed to enter; it shrinks with intimacy and varies with culture, sex and situation. Robert Sommer (1959), in \"Studies in personal space\", and Edward T. Hall (1966), in The Hidden Dimension, made it a research topic. Hall's proxemics described four zones for Americans: intimate (up to 18 inches), personal (18 inches to 4 feet), social (4 to 12 feet) and public (beyond 12 feet). Because people are largely unaware of the distances they keep, they cannot report them reliably, so researchers watch and measure behaviour instead. They record how far apart people sit or stand, and what happens when someone intrudes: Nancy Felipe and Robert Sommer (1966) sat uncomfortably close to people in a mental hospital and a university library and recorded how quickly they moved away. Example: in a nearly empty library, an observer notes that when Kabir sits right beside a stranger, she turns away, builds a wall of books and soon leaves. Option d is right because personal space is studied by observing actual spacing behaviour. Exam trap: sociometry sounds like a measure of social distance, but it maps who chooses whom, not physical distance. Key note: Sommer's 1959 paper appeared in the journal Sociometry, which may have prompted c, but sociometry the method measures social choice, not space; personal space is a matter of observed behaviour, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The survey method collects self-reports from large samples by questionnaire; people are largely unaware of their own spacing, so personal space is not its core concept.", "b": "The interview gathers information by face to face questioning, again self-report; it cannot capture the distances people actually keep.", "c": "Sociometry (Jacob Moreno, 1934) maps attractions and rejections among group members in a sociogram (stars, isolates, cliques); it measures social choice, not physical space."}}, {"q": "Sociology is defined as \"a science which interprets social behaviour with the aim of arriving at a causal explanation of human behaviour\". Whose statement is this?", "concept": "The answer is b: the definition is Max Weber's (1922). In Economy and Society, published after his death, Weber wrote that sociology is a science which attempts the interpretive understanding of social action in order to arrive at a causal explanation of its course and effects. Two ideas sit in that sentence. Social action is behaviour to which the actor attaches a meaning and which takes account of other people. Interpretive understanding, Verstehen, means grasping that meaning from the actor's point of view, rather than only counting outward events. Weber then insisted that this understanding must lead to causal explanation, so that sociology remains a science and not mere description. His approach is called interpretive sociology. Example: to explain why Riya's family chooses an arranged marriage, a Weberian sociologist first asks what the choice means to them (duty, family honour, security) and then explains how such shared meanings produce the wider social pattern. Option b is right because interpretive understanding leading to causal explanation is the core of Weber's definition. Exam trap: Auguste Comte (1838) is the reflex answer because he coined the word sociology, but his positivism sought social laws through observation, not the interpretation of meaning.", "traps": {"a": "Auguste Comte coined the word sociology (1838) and founded positivism, which sought social laws by observation, not Weber's interpretive understanding of meaningful action.", "c": "Georg Simmel (1908) founded formal sociology, the study of the forms of social interaction such as conflict, exchange, the dyad and the triad; the interpretive and causal definition is not his.", "d": "Jones is a filler name here; no standard definition of sociology is attributed to a Jones."}}, {"q": "Most important characteristic of a group leader is:", "concept": "The answer is b: among these options, intelligence is the characteristic most consistently linked with leadership. The trait approach, or great-person theory, asked which personal qualities set leaders apart. Ralph Stogdill (1948) reviewed 124 studies and found that leaders tended to exceed other members in intelligence, alertness, dependability, sociability and activity, though no single trait guaranteed leadership in every situation. Robert Lord, Christy De Vader and George Alliger (1986) re-analysed the evidence by meta-analysis and found intelligence to be the strongest trait correlate of who is perceived as a leader. Intelligence helps a leader understand the task, solve problems, judge situations and plan for the group. Texts add that leaders are usually brighter than their followers, but not by too wide a margin, since a large gap can hamper communication. Example: in a college project group, members turn to Arjun, who grasps the problem quickly and explains it clearly, rather than to the student who controls the budget. Option b is right because intelligence is the personal trait that research repeatedly ties to leadership. Exam trap: access to resources sounds powerful, but it is a source of power attached to a position, not a characteristic of the leader as a person. Key note: Stogdill also showed no single trait guarantees leadership and the situation matters, but among these options intelligence is the trait the research singles out, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Skill at the job is technical competence, useful for an expert or supervisor, but trait research ties emergence as a leader more closely to general intelligence than to task skill.", "c": "Access to resources is a source of power (reward power in John French and Bertram Raven's 1959 bases of power), a feature of the position, not a personal characteristic of the leader.", "d": "Ability to manipulate people is Machiavellianism, a dark trait that can win short-term influence but is not the characteristic research identifies with leaders."}}, {"q": "Whose study demonstrated the influence of a leader on a formal group?", "concept": "The answer is c: the study by E. Paul Torrance (1954) of air force bomber crews demonstrated a leader's influence on a formal group. A formal group has an official structure of positions, ranks and roles set by an organisation. Torrance, the same psychologist who later built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, was then working for the United States Air Force and studied three-man B-26 bomber crews, whose hierarchy was fixed: the pilot (the commander) at the top, the navigator next and the gunner at the bottom. When crews solved problems together, the group decision followed the pilot's view most often, even when the pilot was wrong, while the low-status gunner was least able to get a correct answer accepted. The pattern held even on tasks that had nothing to do with flying, and rank mattered less in temporary crews put together only for the study. His report, \"Some Consequences of Power Differences on Decisions in B-26 Crews\" (1954), showed that formal status and power shape group decisions. Example: in an office team, Meera the junior clerk spots an error in the figures, but the group follows the manager's answer anyway. Option c is right because Torrance's crews showed how the formal leader's position governs group outcomes. Exam trap: Edwin Hollander (1958) is the famous leadership name, so he is the reflex choice; but his work concerns emergent leaders and idiosyncrasy credits, not the influence of rank in formal crews.", "traps": {"a": "Edwin Hollander (1958) proposed idiosyncrasy credits: emergent leaders earn the right to deviate by first conforming and showing competence; that is not the study of leaders in formal crews.", "b": "Morris Ginsberg was a British sociologist at the London School of Economics; no classic study of leaders in formal groups is his, so the name is a filler.", "d": "James Julian wrote with Edwin Hollander (1969) on leadership as a transaction between leader and followers, a theoretical review rather than the formal group study."}}, {"q": "In which communication network is the leader the only person who can talk to any member of the group, while no one else can?", "concept": "The answer is b: in the wheel network the leader at the centre is the only person who can talk to every member, while the others can talk only to the leader. Communication networks are fixed patterns of who may send messages to whom. Alex Bavelas (1950) and Harold Leavitt (1951) studied them by placing five people behind partitions, letting them pass written notes only along set channels, and giving them a shared problem. In the wheel, the most centralised network, every message passes through the hub. On simple tasks it is the fastest and most accurate, and the hub quickly emerges as leader, but members on the spokes are the least satisfied. In the chain, messages pass along a line and the middle person relays. The Y is a chain with a fork at one end, fairly centralised. In the circle each person talks only to two neighbours: slow, with no clear leader, but the highest morale. Later work added the all-channel network, in which everyone talks to everyone. Example: in a tuition centre, Kabir the coordinator receives every teacher's report and passes on every instruction, and the teachers never talk to each other: a wheel. Option b is right because only the wheel gives one central person access to all and denies it to everyone else. Exam trap: the Y network also has a central position, but there other members still have links of their own; only in the wheel does every message go through one person.", "traps": {"a": "In the circle network each member talks to two neighbours, so communication is decentralised and no single person can reach everyone.", "c": "In the chain network messages pass along a line; the middle person relays information, but no one is linked to every member and the end members can each reach only one person.", "d": "The Y network is a chain with a fork; its central person links several members, but other members also have their own links, so it is not the pattern in which the leader alone can talk to all."}}, {"q": "Most important works on attitudes were carried out by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the most important early work on attitudes was carried out by Louis Leon Thurstone. In a famous paper titled \"Attitudes can be measured\" (1928), Thurstone argued that an attitude, a person's favourable or unfavourable evaluation of an object, could be placed on a numerical scale like any other quantity. With Ernest J. Chave in The Measurement of Attitude (1929), measuring attitude towards the church, he devised the method of equal-appearing intervals. In that study 300 judges sorted 130 opinion statements into 11 piles, from most unfavourable to most favourable. Each statement's scale value was its median pile; statements the judges placed inconsistently were dropped, and 45 evenly graded statements formed the final scale. A respondent ticks the statements he or she agrees with, and the score is the average (median or mean) scale value of those ticked. This was the first serious attempt to measure attitudes, and it opened the whole field of attitude scaling. Example: Riya's score on a Thurstone scale of attitude towards women's education is the average scale value of the statements she endorses. Option c is right because Thurstone pioneered attitude measurement and the other names built on his foundation. Exam trap: the summated rating scale of Rensis Likert (1932) is the one used everywhere today, so it feels more important. Key note: Likert's scale is more widely used today, but Thurstone's work is the foundational one that first showed attitudes could be measured, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Allen Edwards (1957) wrote Techniques of Attitude Scale Construction and developed the scale discrimination technique, which combined earlier methods; he systematised Thurstone's groundwork rather than founding it.", "b": "Rensis Likert (1932) introduced the simpler summated rating scale, now the most used; it came after, and simplified, Thurstone's pioneering method.", "d": "Robert Park was a Chicago school sociologist whose idea of social distance Emory Bogardus (1925) turned into a scale; he did not carry out the major work on attitude measurement."}}, {"q": "Likert-scale is normally a:", "concept": "The answer is a: a Likert scale is normally a five-valued (five-point) measure. Rensis Likert (1932), in \"A technique for the measurement of attitudes\", introduced the method of summated ratings. The researcher writes a set of opinion statements about the attitude object, some favourable and some unfavourable. Each respondent rates every statement on five ordered alternatives: strongly agree, agree, undecided, disagree, strongly disagree. Responses are scored 5 to 1 for favourable statements and reversed (1 to 5) for unfavourable ones, so that a high total always means a favourable attitude. The item scores are then added with equal weights, hence \"summated\". No panel of judges is needed, unlike the method of Louis Leon Thurstone (1928); items are kept by item analysis if high and low total scorers answer them differently. Likert scales are easy to build and usually as reliable as Thurstone scales, which is why they dominate questionnaires. Example: Arjun marks \"agree\" (scored 4) on \"Exams motivate me to study\" and \"strongly disagree\" on \"Exams are a waste of time\", which reverses to 5. Option a is right because the standard Likert format has five response points. Exam trap: seven-point versions are common in modern research, so b looks plausible; but the classic, textbook Likert item is five-point.", "traps": {"b": "Seven-point agreement scales are a later variant used for finer discrimination; the normal, original Likert format has five points.", "c": "A three-point scale (agree, undecided, disagree) is a simplified form used with children or quick surveys, not the standard Likert scale.", "d": "A two-valued (yes or no, agree or disagree) format is a dichotomous checklist, which loses the graded intensity the Likert method was built to capture."}}, {"q": "Which one do you consider most important for determining interpersonal attraction?", "concept": "The answer is b: proximity, physical nearness, is the factor texts call most important in determining who becomes attracted to whom. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) studied Westgate West, married-student housing at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where flats were assigned at random. Residents named 41 percent of their next-door neighbours as close friends, against 22 percent of those two doors away and only 10 percent of those at the far end of the corridor. Functional distance mattered too: people living near stairways and mailboxes made more friends. Proximity works because it brings repeated exposure, and Robert Zajonc (1968) showed through the mere exposure effect that familiarity alone raises liking; it also gives people the chance to interact, discover similarities and exchange rewards. Without some contact, no other factor can operate. Example: Kabir's closest friends in his first hostel year are the boys on his own floor, not the ones he would have picked by interests. Option b is right because proximity is the first and most powerful precondition for attraction. Exam trap: similarity is a strong predictor once people have met (Donn Byrne, 1971), so it is the popular wrong choice. Key note: similarity and reward are close rivals, and reward theory explains why the others work; but texts usually call proximity the single most powerful predictor of who becomes friends, since attraction cannot start without contact, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Similarity of interests and attitudes (Donn Byrne's law of attraction, 1971) is a strong predictor once people have met, but proximity has to bring them together first.", "c": "Complementarity, the idea that opposites attract by meeting each other's needs (Robert Winch, 1958), has weak research support compared with proximity and similarity.", "d": "Rewardingness is the reinforcement-affect model (Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore, 1970): we like those who reward us; it explains why other factors work but is not the factor texts rank first."}}, {"q": "Which one is the most populated country in the world?", "concept": "The answer is c: China was the most populous country in the world in 2010, the year this question dates from. China's sixth national census, taken in November 2010, counted about 1.34 billion people on the mainland. India was second: its 2011 census counted about 1.21 billion. The United States, third, had about 309 million at its 2010 census, and Russia about 143 million, so neither came close. China's growth had slowed sharply after the one-child policy of around 1980, while India's population kept growing faster because of higher fertility, so the gap between the two was steadily closing. Population size matters to psychologists and social scientists because density and crowding affect stress, health and social behaviour, and population pressure shapes education, employment and migration. Example: when Meera studied geography in 2010, her textbook listed China first and India a close second, with India expected to pass China within a few decades. Option c is right because, in 2010, China's population exceeded that of every other country. Exam trap: India is the right answer to an up-to-date question, so anyone who follows recent news may pick it for this old item. Key note: this answer is correct for 2010. The United Nations (World Population Prospects 2022) estimated that India overtook China around April 2023, so if this item appears in a current exam the answer is India; for this 2010 paper, c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The USA was the third most populous country in 2010 (about 309 million at its census), far behind China and India.", "b": "Russia is the largest country by area, not by population; it had only about 143 million people in 2010.", "d": "India was second in 2010 (about 1.21 billion at its 2011 census); it overtook China only around April 2023, according to United Nations estimates."}}, {"q": "The major agenda of the Copenhagen conference 2009 was:", "concept": "The answer is d: the major agenda of the Copenhagen conference of December 2009 was global warming. The meeting was COP15, the 15th Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC, adopted in 1992). Its aim was to agree a successor to the Kyoto Protocol (1997), whose first commitment period ran to 2012, with binding cuts in the greenhouse gases (carbon dioxide, methane and others) that trap heat in the atmosphere and raise global temperatures. The talks did not produce a binding treaty. Instead, a small group of countries, including the United States, China, India, Brazil and South Africa, drafted the Copenhagen Accord, which the conference only \"took note\" of. The Accord recognised that warming should be kept below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels, asked countries to pledge emission targets, and promised climate finance for developing countries. Example: Arjun, writing a newspaper summary, would headline the meeting as a climate change summit, not a population or pollution conference. Option d is right because the conference was held under the climate change convention to tackle global warming. Exam trap: environmental degradation and industrial emission control both sound right because they are related; but the first is far broader than the agenda, and the second is only one means to the climate goal.", "traps": {"a": "Population explosion is the subject of United Nations population conferences such as the International Conference on Population and Development (Cairo, 1994), not the Copenhagen climate meeting.", "b": "Environmental degradation is a much broader theme (soil, forests, water, biodiversity), covered by meetings such as the 1992 Rio Earth Summit; Copenhagen focused specifically on climate.", "c": "Industrial emission control is one means of cutting greenhouse gases, but the agenda was the goal itself, limiting global warming, across all sources including energy, transport and deforestation."}}, {"q": "Which school of psychology focuses on psychodynamics?", "concept": "The answer is c: psychodynamics is the central idea of the psychoanalytic school. Psychodynamics means the study of mental forces in motion: unconscious wishes, drives and conflicts that push against each other and against the demands of reality and conscience, while defence mechanisms keep the struggle out of awareness. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) built psychoanalysis on this idea. He pictured the mind as id, ego and superego in constant tension, and he held that symptoms, slips of the tongue and dreams are compromises between a forbidden wish and the forces that block it. Example: Kabir keeps 'forgetting' his dentist appointment; a psychodynamic reading says an unconscious fear is quietly winning over his conscious intention. Followers and rivals who kept this dynamic view, such as Alfred Adler and Carl Jung (who broke with Freud between 1911 and 1913), the ego psychologists and the object relations theorists, are grouped together as the psychodynamic approach. Option c is right because psychoanalysis is the only school whose core concern is the dynamic play of unconscious forces. The other schools explain behaviour without inner conflict: behaviourism through learned stimulus-response links, Gestalt psychology through the organisation of perception, and humanistic psychology through growth toward self-actualisation. Exam trap: the word 'dynamic' also appears in Kurt Lewin's (1935) A Dynamic Theory of Personality and in Robert Woodworth's (1918) Dynamic Psychology, so it is easy to link psychodynamics to Gestalt or field theory; the term psychodynamics belongs to Freud's tradition.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), studies only observable behaviour and how it is learned through conditioning; it rejects unconscious forces, the very stuff of psychodynamics.", "b": "Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) studies how perception organises parts into meaningful wholes; its concern is perceptual organisation, not unconscious drives and conflicts.", "d": "Humanistic psychology (Abraham Maslow, Carl Rogers), the 'third force' of the 1950s and 1960s, arose as a reaction against psychoanalysis and stresses free will, growth and self-actualisation rather than unconscious conflict."}}, {"q": "Human behaviour is largely regulated by:", "concept": "The answer is a: human behaviour is largely regulated by the central nervous system. The nervous system has two main parts. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is all the nerves outside them; it divides into the somatic system (sensory nerves carrying messages in, motor nerves carrying commands out to the skeletal muscles) and the autonomic nervous system (ANS), which serves the internal organs. The CNS is the control centre. It receives sensory messages, integrates them with memory, motives and plans, and sends out the commands that produce movement, speech, thought and emotional expression. The spinal cord handles simple reflexes and relays messages; the brain does the higher work. Even the ANS is steered by the CNS, mainly through the hypothalamus and the brainstem. Example: when Meera hears her name called on a crowded bus, her ears send signals inward, her cortex recognises her friend's voice, and her brain directs her to turn and answer; every step is organised centrally. Option a is right because the brain and spinal cord integrate input and direct nearly all behaviour, voluntary and involuntary. Exam trap: the ANS sounds 'autonomous', so it is easy to think it runs behaviour on its own; in fact it controls only internal organs such as the heart, glands and gut, and its two branches, sympathetic and parasympathetic, are themselves regulated by the brain.", "traps": {"b": "The autonomic nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that controls involuntary functions (heart rate, digestion, sweating); it regulates internal organs, not behaviour as a whole, and is itself controlled by the hypothalamus.", "c": "Sensory stimulation is input that the nervous system receives and interprets; it can trigger behaviour but does not regulate it, since the same stimulus produces different actions depending on central processing.", "d": "The parasympathetic nervous system is only one branch of the ANS, the 'rest and digest' system that slows the heart and aids digestion; it is far too narrow to regulate behaviour in general."}}, {"q": "\"Inferiority complex is a biosocial phenomenon\": who said this?", "concept": "The answer is b: Alfred Adler described the inferiority complex as a biosocial phenomenon. Alfred Adler (1907, Study of Organ Inferiority) founded individual psychology after leaving Sigmund Freud's circle in 1911. He began with the body: a weak organ (poor eyesight, a stammer, a frail chest) makes a person feel inferior and drives him to compensate, sometimes so well that the weakness becomes a strength. He then widened the idea. Every child starts small, dependent and less capable than the adults around him, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. How these feelings develop depends on social life: comparison with siblings and peers, birth order, pampering or neglect, and the growth of social interest. Because the roots are both biological (the body, childhood helplessness) and social (family, comparison with others), the phenomenon is biosocial. When inferiority feelings become exaggerated and paralysing, they form an inferiority complex; covering them with arrogance is the superiority complex. Healthy striving for superiority, guided by social interest, is the normal way out. Example: Arjun, short and teased at school, works hard at debating and becomes the school's best speaker; that is compensation. Option b is right because the inferiority concept, its bodily origin and its social shaping are all Adler's. Exam trap: the word 'complex' is easily linked with Carl Jung, who used it for clusters of emotionally charged ideas found in his word association tests; but the inferiority complex is Adler's.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud explained personality through sexual and aggressive drives and the Oedipus complex; inferiority feelings were not central to his theory, and Adler left him partly over this disagreement.", "c": "Carl Jung wrote about feeling-toned complexes and the collective unconscious with its archetypes; the inferiority complex and its biosocial roots are Adler's idea, not his.", "d": "Erich Fromm (Escape from Freedom, 1941) explained personality through social character and the urge to escape the loneliness of freedom; he did not describe inferiority as a biosocial complex."}}, {"q": "C. G. Jung proposed the theory of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Carl Gustav Jung proposed the theory of the collective unconscious. Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961), a Swiss psychiatrist, was Sigmund Freud's closest early ally but broke with him in 1913 and founded analytical psychology. Jung kept Freud's idea of a personal unconscious, the layer that holds each person's forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes. Beneath it he placed a deeper, inherited layer common to all humanity: the collective unconscious. It contains archetypes, inborn tendencies to perceive and respond in typical ways, which appear in myths, dreams, religions and folk tales across cultures. Key archetypes are the persona (the social mask), the shadow (the dark, rejected side), the anima and animus (the feminine side in men and the masculine side in women), the great mother, the wise old man and the self (the archetype of wholeness). So Jung's psyche has three layers: the conscious ego, the personal unconscious and the collective unconscious. Example: Riya dreams of a wise old guide on a mountain, a figure she has never met; Jung would read the figure as an archetype rising from the collective unconscious, not as a personal memory. Option b is right because the collective unconscious is Jung's distinctive contribution. Exam trap: option a looks right because Jung wrote a great deal about the unconscious, but the theory of the unconscious as such is Freud's; only the collective, inherited layer is Jung's.", "traps": {"a": "The theory of the unconscious (with the preconscious and the conscious) is Sigmund Freud's (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900); Jung accepted it as the personal unconscious and added the collective layer beneath it.", "c": "'Subconscious' is a popular term used by Pierre Janet (1889); Freud rejected it as vague, and it is not Jung's theory.", "d": "'Ethical unconscious' is not a theory in any school; it is a filler. Moral control in Freud's model belongs to the superego, which is partly unconscious."}}, {"q": "16PF scale is based on the views of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the 16PF scale is based on the trait theory of Raymond Cattell. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) wanted to find the basic units of personality scientifically rather than by intuition. He began with Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert's (1936) list of 17,953 trait words from the dictionary, kept the roughly 4,500 that name stable traits, merged synonyms into 171 terms, and then applied factor analysis, a statistical method that finds which measures vary together. He used three kinds of data: L-data (life records and ratings by others), Q-data (self-report questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests). The result was 16 source traits, the building blocks of personality, published as the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) in 1949. The factors include warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, liveliness, rule-consciousness, social boldness, sensitivity, vigilance, abstractedness, privateness, apprehension, openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism and tension. Example: Kabir's 16PF profile shows high dominance and low apprehension; his counsellor reads the 16 scores as a map of his source traits when advising him on careers. Option c is right because the 16PF is Cattell's own factor-analytic inventory. Exam trap: Gordon Allport also worked with trait words, and Cattell started from Allport's list, so a is tempting; but Allport preferred studying the unique individual, distrusted factor analysis and never built the 16PF.", "traps": {"a": "Gordon Allport (1937) described cardinal, central and secondary traits and studied the individual idiographically; he did not build a factor-analytic questionnaire like the 16PF.", "b": "Hans Eysenck also used factor analysis but arrived at only three broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism, psychoticism), measured by the EPI (1964, extraversion and neuroticism only) and the EPQ (1975, which added psychoticism), not 16 factors.", "d": "Carl Rogers (1951) built self theory and used the Q-sort to compare the real and ideal self; he was a humanistic therapist, not a factor-analytic trait theorist."}}, {"q": "\"Central\" and \"Surface\" traits are the components of:", "concept": "The answer is d: surface and central (source) traits are the components of Raymond Cattell's system. Raymond Cattell (1950, Personality: A Systematic Theoretical and Factual Study) separated two levels of traits. Surface traits are clusters of behaviours that tend to go together and can be seen directly, such as a person who is helpful, polite, generous and patient. They are many and fairly unstable. Source traits are the deeper, central underlying factors that cause the surface clusters; they can be found only by factor analysis, they are stable, and the 16PF measures 16 of them. Cattell also classed traits by content: ability traits, temperament traits and dynamic traits (ergs, or innate drives, and learned sentiments). Example: Meera's friends see her as talkative, cheerful and fond of parties; these surface behaviours stem from a single source trait, which Cattell would call high liveliness and, at the second-order level, extraversion. Option d is right because the surface versus source distinction is unique to Cattell, and the 'central' traits in the stem can only mean his source traits, the core factors beneath the surface. Exam trap: Gordon Allport (1937) used the term 'central traits' for the 5 to 10 traits that best describe a person, alongside cardinal and secondary traits, so a looks tempting. Key note: 'central' is Allport's word, so the stem mixes the two systems; but surface traits belong only to Cattell, whose source traits are the central, underlying ones, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Gordon Allport divided traits into cardinal, central and secondary; he did use 'central', but he never spoke of surface traits, so his system cannot supply both terms in the stem.", "b": "Sigmund Freud's system is structural (id, ego, superego) and developmental (oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital stages); it has no trait components at all.", "c": "Alfred Adler's individual psychology rests on inferiority, compensation, striving for superiority, style of life and social interest, not on a classification of traits."}}, {"q": "Abnormality is primarily a:", "concept": "The answer is b: abnormality is primarily a matter of quantitative extremism. The continuity view, also called the dimensional or statistical view, holds that normal and abnormal behaviour differ in degree, not in kind. Everyone has some anxiety, sadness, suspicion and fussiness about order; abnormality is the same quality pushed to an extreme, too intense, too frequent or too long-lasting. On the normal curve most people cluster around the average, and the statistical criterion calls behaviour abnormal when it lies far out in the tails, for example beyond two standard deviations from the mean. Example: Kabir checks the door lock once before leaving, as most people do; his cousin checks it forty times and is late every day. The behaviour is the same; only the amount differs, and the extreme amount is what makes it abnormal. This view underlies rating scales for anxiety and depression and the dimensional model of personality disorders in Section III of DSM-5 (2013). Option b is right because the standard teaching treats abnormality as an extreme position on dimensions shared by everyone. Exam trap: option a sounds scholarly, but 'qualitative' means different in kind, which is the opposing discontinuity (disease) view; c and d are invented phrases. Key note: some severe disorders, such as psychotic states, do look qualitatively different, and DSM and ICD diagnoses are categorical; but the standard teaching is that abnormality is mainly a quantitative deviation, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Qualitative disequilibrium expresses the discontinuity or disease view, that abnormal behaviour is different in kind from normal behaviour; it is the position the continuity view rejects.", "c": "'Mental derailment' is not a textbook term for abnormality; derailment is a specific formal thought disorder (loosening of associations) seen in schizophrenia, not a definition of abnormality.", "d": "'Conscious diffusion' is a filler with no meaning in abnormal psychology; it may echo Erik Erikson's 'identity diffusion', which is a developmental crisis, not abnormality in general."}}, {"q": "Which model may explain the concept of \"Anxiety Neurosis\" in a clearer way?", "concept": "The answer is c: the psychoanalytic model explains anxiety neurosis most clearly. The term itself is psychoanalytic. Sigmund Freud (1895) separated 'anxiety neurosis' from the broad category of neurasthenia, describing general irritability, anxious expectation and anxiety attacks. His first explanation was that undischarged sexual tension turned into anxiety. In Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) he recast it: anxiety is the ego's danger signal that a forbidden id impulse is about to break through, so neurotic anxiety is fear of one's own instincts. The ego responds with repression and other defence mechanisms; when they fail, free-floating anxiety and symptoms appear. Freud distinguished realistic anxiety (from real danger), neurotic anxiety (from the id) and moral anxiety (from the superego). Example: Riya feels a constant, vague dread with no clear cause; a psychoanalyst would trace it to an unacceptable wish she has repressed and help her bring it into awareness. Option c is right because both the concept of anxiety neurosis and its classic explanation come from psychoanalysis. Exam trap: d (learning theory) is a strong rival because conditioning explains phobias well, but anxiety neurosis as a concept is Freud's, and learning theory explains specific fears more clearly than diffuse neurotic anxiety. Key note: today generalised anxiety is explained by cognitive behavioural and biological models too, but the concept of anxiety neurosis comes from psychoanalysis, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The medical model treats mental disorder as an illness with symptoms, diagnosis and physical treatment (Emil Kraepelin, 1899); it names and treats anxiety but does not explain the neurotic conflict behind it.", "b": "The organic model looks for brain lesions, chemical imbalances or other bodily causes; neurosis was defined as a disorder without organic damage, so this model explains it least well.", "d": "The learning theory model (O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory, 1947: fear is classically conditioned and kept alive by avoidance) explains phobias well, but anxiety neurosis as a concept comes from Freud."}}, {"q": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder is generally caused by:", "concept": "The answer is c: older texts teach that obsessive-compulsive disorder is caused mainly by psychoemotional factors. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) has two parts: obsessions, unwanted intrusive thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety (fear of dirt, of harming someone, of blasphemy), and compulsions, repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety (washing, checking, counting, praying). Sufferers usually know the thoughts are excessive but cannot stop them. In the psychoanalytic account, Sigmund Freud (1909, the Rat Man case) saw obsessional neurosis as a conflict over aggressive and sexual impulses, later traced to the anal stage, held in check by defences such as isolation, undoing and reaction formation; the rituals 'undo' the forbidden wish. In this view the cause is psychological and emotional: anxiety from inner conflict. Example: Arjun has a sudden thought of pushing his brother and then must touch the door frame seven times to feel safe; the ritual soothes the anxiety the thought created. Option c is right because the classical textbook model traces OCD to psychoemotional conflict and anxiety. Exam trap: d is tempting, since modern cognitive models stress thinking errors such as inflated responsibility (Paul Salkovskis, 1985), but the item follows the older psychogenic account. Key note: modern research shows OCD has no single cause; twin studies suggest it is moderately heritable (roughly 40 to 50 per cent), circuits linking the orbitofrontal cortex, striatum and thalamus are involved, and serotonin-acting drugs help; among these options, psychoemotional factors is the textbook answer for this old item, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Hereditary factors play a real but partial role (OCD runs in families and is moderately heritable), yet no text says OCD is generally caused by heredity alone.", "b": "Environmental factors (stress, life events, a strict or overcontrolling upbringing) can trigger or shape OCD, but on their own they are too broad to be the general cause the item asks for.", "d": "Cognitive factors (inflated responsibility, thought-action fusion, need for certainty) are the focus of modern cognitive behavioural models, not the classical psychoemotional account this item follows."}}, {"q": "De-addiction centres are meant for:", "concept": "The answer is a: de-addiction centres are meant for the treatment of drug abuse. A de-addiction centre treats people with substance use problems: alcohol, opioids (heroin, prescription painkillers), cannabis, tobacco, sedatives and other drugs. Treatment usually runs in stages. First comes detoxification, medically managed withdrawal, in which doctors ease symptoms such as tremors, sweating and craving. Then comes the harder work: counselling, motivational interviewing, family therapy, relapse prevention (G. Alan Marlatt and Judith Gordon, 1985, who taught clients to cope with high-risk situations), and rehabilitation to help the person return to work and family life. In India such centres are run by government hospitals under the Health Ministry's Drug De-addiction Programme, by the National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre of AIIMS, by rehabilitation centres for addicts supported by the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, and by NGOs. Example: Kabir, dependent on alcohol for five years, spends ten days in a de-addiction ward for detoxification and then attends weekly counselling and a self-help group to stay sober. Option a is right because the purpose of these centres is the treatment of substance abuse and dependence. Exam trap: addiction overlaps with crime, panic and trauma, so options about delinquency or PTSD sound plausible; but a de-addiction centre is defined by its target, substance use, not by the problems that may accompany it.", "traps": {"b": "Delinquency control belongs to the juvenile justice system (observation homes, special homes, probation), which deals with children in conflict with the law, not with addiction treatment as such.", "c": "Panic disorder, with sudden unexpected panic attacks and worry about further attacks, is treated in mental health clinics with CBT (including interoceptive exposure) and medication, not in de-addiction centres.", "d": "PTSD management (trauma-focused CBT, prolonged exposure, EMDR) takes place in mental health and trauma services; substance misuse can accompany PTSD, but de-addiction centres target the substance use."}}, {"q": "Human behaviour cannot be subjected to experimentation primarily because of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the main reason given for why human behaviour resists experimentation is the predominance of subjectivity. An experiment needs variables that can be observed, measured and controlled. Much of human behaviour, though, depends on inner states such as thoughts, feelings, intentions, values and free choice, which are private and known directly only to the person. Subjectivity enters from three sides. First, the participant's inner life cannot be observed directly. Second, the participant reacts to being studied: in the Hawthorne studies (Western Electric, 1924 to 1932) workers' output was reported to rise whatever was changed, apparently because they knew they were being watched, and Martin Orne (1962) showed that subjects pick up demand characteristics, cues about what the experimenter wants, and play along. Third, the experimenter's own expectations colour the results (Robert Rosenthal, 1966, experimenter expectancy effects). Example: Meera, in a memory experiment, guesses the hypothesis and tries harder in one condition, so her score reflects her guess rather than the independent variable. Option b is right because subjectivity is the root problem from which the other listed difficulties follow. Exam trap: c and d (conceptual vagueness, limited operationalisation) sound technical, but they are consequences of subjectivity, not the primary reason. Key note: psychology does run controlled experiments on humans, so this is the classic objection the item asks about, not a settled fact; among the options the root reason given in texts is subjectivity, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Extreme variation in experimental findings is a result of uncontrolled factors, not a reason in principle why behaviour cannot be experimented on; good design and replication reduce it.", "c": "Conceptual vagueness (fuzzy concepts like 'will' or 'happiness') follows from the private, subjective nature of behaviour; it is a consequence, not the primary cause.", "d": "Limited operationalisation, the difficulty of turning an inner state into a measurable operation (Percy Bridgman, 1927, operational definitions), also stems from subjectivity; operational definitions are the remedy, not the root problem."}}, {"q": "Which of the following situations demonstrates a functional relationship between variables?", "concept": "The answer is b: Y = f(X) expresses a functional relationship between variables. In mathematics a function is a rule that links each value of one variable to a value of another. Y = f(X) is read 'Y is a function of X': Y depends on X, so when X changes, Y changes in a lawful way. In an experiment X is the independent variable (IV), the factor the researcher manipulates, and Y is the dependent variable (DV), the behaviour measured; the aim of the experiment is to discover the form of f, the rule linking them. The best-known psychological example is Kurt Lewin's (1936) formula B = f(P, E): behaviour is a function of the person and the environment. Psychophysics works the same way: Gustav Fechner's (1860) law says sensation is a function of the logarithm of stimulus intensity. Example: Arjun varies the hours of sleep students get before a test (X) and measures their scores (Y); if scores rise with sleep up to a point, he has found a functional relationship, scores = f(sleep). Option b is right because only b uses the function sign f, which states that Y depends on X. Exam trap: a, Y = (X), looks almost the same but simply says Y equals X, an identity with no rule of dependence, while c and d are products in which Y appears on both sides.", "traps": {"a": "Y = (X) states only that Y equals X, an identity; it shows no rule by which Y depends on X, so it is not a functional statement.", "c": "Y = (Y·X) puts Y on both sides as a product with X; it defines Y in terms of itself and shows no dependence of Y on X.", "d": "Y = (X·Y) is the same product written in reverse order; it also puts Y on both sides and states no functional rule."}}, {"q": "An experiment shall be subjected to a gross error when:", "concept": "The answer is a: an experiment suffers a gross error when the DV is under the influence of an extraneous variable. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV), hoping to show that the IV caused any change. An extraneous variable is any other factor that could affect the DV: time of day, noise, participants' age, practice, fatigue, the experimenter's manner. If such a factor varies along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable, and the result can no longer be attributed to the IV. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the main threats to internal validity: history, maturation, testing, instrumentation, statistical regression, selection, experimental mortality and their interactions. This is a systematic (gross) error, not a small random one, because it biases the result in one direction. Example: Meera tests a new teaching method on a morning class and the old method on a tired afternoon class; the morning class scores higher, but time of day, not the method, may explain it. Controls include randomisation, elimination, holding conditions constant, matching, counterbalancing and building the variable into the design. Option a is right because uncontrolled extraneous influence on the DV destroys the causal conclusion. Exam trap: d (mood fluctuations) is one example of an extraneous variable, so it looks right, but it is only one case; a states the general rule.", "traps": {"b": "Reducing the IV to newer concepts is not a recognised source of error; refining the IV through operational definition actually improves an experiment.", "c": "Sample size affects the precision and power of a study (sampling error), but a 'deviating' sample size is not what makes changes in the DV unattributable to the IV.", "d": "Mood fluctuations are one possible extraneous (organismic) variable; they cause gross error only when they act on the DV uncontrolled, which is the general rule stated in a."}}, {"q": "Perceptual constancy refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: perceptual constancy means seeing objects as unchanged despite a change in sensory information. The image an object casts on the retina changes all the time: it shrinks as the object moves away, changes shape as the object tilts, and changes in brightness and colour as the light changes. Yet we perceive the object itself as stable. Size constancy: a friend walking away does not seem to shrink, because the brain scales the retinal image by perceived distance (Emil Emmert, 1881, showed this with afterimages). Shape constancy: a door swinging open casts a trapezoid image but still looks rectangular. Brightness and colour constancy: white paper looks white in sunlight and in shade, and a red apple looks red under yellowish lamplight. Constancy depends on cues such as distance, context and familiarity; when the cues are removed or falsified, as in the Ames room of Adelbert Ames Jr., it breaks down. Example: Kabir sees his mother waving from the far end of a long railway platform; her retinal image is tiny, but he perceives her as her normal size. Option b is right because constancy is defined by stable perception against changing sensory input. Exam trap: c sounds similar but says the object's form and shape change; in constancy the object does not change, only its image on the retina does, and c also covers only shape.", "traps": {"a": "Seeing no change in an object shown again and again describes habituation or familiarity, a response to repetition, not constancy in the face of changing sensory input.", "c": "This reverses the idea: in constancy the object's real form stays the same and only its retinal image changes; the option also covers shape constancy alone, leaving out size, brightness and colour.", "d": "'Under any illumination' is too absolute: brightness and colour constancy hold across ordinary lighting changes but fail in extreme or unusual light, and the option leaves out size and shape constancy."}}, {"q": "Which one is not considered as a law of perceptual organization?", "concept": "The answer is c: specificity is not a law of perceptual organisation. Gestalt psychologists, led by Max Wertheimer (1923, 'Laws of organisation in perceptual forms'), showed that we do not see scattered parts but organised wholes: the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The brain groups elements by a set of laws. Proximity (nearness): items close together are seen as a group. Many Indian texts add contiguity, nearness in space or time, as a closely related factor, so events that occur together are seen as belonging together. Similarity: alike items (same colour, shape or size) group. Closure: gaps are filled so that complete figures are seen. Continuity (good continuation): elements on a smooth line or curve group. Common fate: items moving together group. Figure-ground: one part stands out as a figure against a background. Above all stands Prägnanz: we see the simplest, most stable organisation the conditions allow. Example: Riya looks at a row of dots spaced in pairs and sees four pairs rather than eight separate dots; that is proximity. Option c is right because specificity names no grouping principle; the word belongs to other areas, such as the doctrine of specific nerve energies of Johannes Muller (Handbook of Human Physiology, 1833 to 1840) or Charles Spearman's (1904) specific factors. Exam trap: contiguity sounds like a learning term (Edwin Guthrie's contiguity theory, 1935), so a looks like the odd one out; but in perception texts contiguity is listed as a grouping factor alongside proximity. Key note: Max Wertheimer's own list does not use the word contiguity, so a purist might pick a; but contiguity is nearness in space or time, a form of proximity, while specificity names no grouping principle at all, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Contiguity, nearness in space or time, is listed in many Indian texts as a grouping factor close to proximity: things that occur together are seen as belonging together, so it is a law of organisation.", "b": "Proximity is a core Gestalt law (Max Wertheimer, 1923): elements close together are grouped, as when spaced dots are seen in pairs.", "d": "Similarity is a core Gestalt law: elements alike in colour, shape or size are grouped, as when alternating rows of circles and squares are seen as rows rather than columns."}}, {"q": "Reaction time is defined as:", "concept": "The answer is b: reaction time is the time elapsing between the onset of a stimulus and the beginning of a response. Reaction time (RT) is one of the oldest measures in experimental psychology. Franciscus Donders (1868) used it to time mental processes by subtraction. In simple RT there is one stimulus and one response (press a key when a light appears); in go/no-go RT the person responds to one stimulus but not to another; in choice RT different stimuli need different responses. Subtracting one RT from another timed the added mental stage: go/no-go minus simple RT gave the time to identify the stimulus, and choice minus go/no-go the time to choose the response. RT is measured from the instant the stimulus appears to the instant the response begins, not to when it ends; for a simple reaction to a light it is roughly a fifth of a second, and a little faster to sound. RT lengthens with more choices (Hick's law, William Hick, 1952), with fatigue, alcohol and age, and shortens with practice, alertness and stronger stimuli. Example: in a laboratory practical Arjun presses a key as soon as a buzzer sounds; the chronoscope records 0.17 seconds between the buzzer's onset and his finger starting to move. Option b is right because it gives the full textbook definition, fixing both ends of the interval: onset of the stimulus and beginning of the response. Exam trap: c and d describe speed of responding in general, not a measured interval between two defined points. Key note: a says the same thing in fewer words, so both a and b are accurate; b is the textbook wording, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is the same definition in shortened form and is accurate in substance; it loses only because b gives the full textbook wording, 'time elapsing between the onset of a stimulus and the beginning of a response'.", "c": "'Responding time of an individual' is vague: it does not say where the interval starts or ends, and could include the whole duration of the movement, which RT excludes.", "d": "'Fastness of a response' describes speed in general, a quality, not the measured time interval between stimulus onset and response onset."}}, {"q": "Span of attention is susceptible to wider fluctuations:", "concept": "The answer is a: the span of attention shows its widest fluctuations during childhood. Span of attention has two senses in textbooks. One is the number of items a person can grasp in a single brief glance: William Stanley Jevons (1871) found he never erred with three or four beans, and tachistoscope studies put the adult span at about six to eight unrelated items. The other is how long attention can stay fixed on one task. Both are small and unstable in young children. A child's attention is captured by every new sound, colour or movement (external determinants dominate), the frontal lobes that support voluntary, sustained attention are still maturing, and interests change quickly. So the child's attention jumps from object to object and lasts only a short time. With age, voluntary control grows, interests become stable, and the span widens and steadies. Adulthood shows the most stable span; old age brings some narrowing of divided and sustained attention, but not the wide swings of childhood. Example: five-year-old Meera starts a jigsaw, drops it to chase a cat, returns, then wanders off to the television, all within ten minutes; her teacher plans short, varied activities for this reason. Option a is right because instability of attention is greatest in childhood. Exam trap: fluctuation of attention in the narrow sense (attention to a faint, unchanging stimulus waxes and wanes every few seconds) occurs at every age; the item asks about developmental instability, which is widest in childhood. Key note: the stem loosely joins span and fluctuation, and attention also grows less steady in old age; but the child's attention is the most easily captured and shifted, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "In adolescence attention becomes much more voluntary and sustained as stable interests form and the frontal lobes mature, so its swings are smaller than in childhood.", "c": "Adulthood shows the widest and most stable span of attention, so it is the opposite of the answer.", "d": "Old age brings some decline in divided and sustained attention and slower processing, but the span narrows gradually rather than fluctuating widely as it does in childhood."}}, {"q": "Which score on the following variables can be treated as continuous?", "concept": "The answer is a: scores on an intelligence test can be treated as continuous. A continuous variable can, in principle, take any value within a range, including fractions: height, weight, time, temperature, and psychological traits such as intelligence, anxiety and aptitude. Between any two values there is always another possible value. A discrete variable takes only separate, whole values, usually counts: number of children, books, trees or hairs. Nobody has 3.5 trees. Intelligence is assumed to be a continuous trait spread smoothly across people, so test scores are treated as continuous even though they are reported in whole numbers. Henry Garrett (1926, Statistics in Psychology and Education) explains that each score stands for an interval: a score of 45 covers 44.5 to 45.5, its exact limits. This is why class intervals, histograms and the normal curve can be applied to test scores. Example: Kabir scores 108 on an intelligence test and his friend scores 109; we can imagine an ability level of 108.4 between them, even if the test cannot show it, but Kabir cannot own 108.4 psychology books. Option a is right because intelligence is a continuous trait measured on a scale with no gaps in principle. Exam trap: d (hairs on a head) involves huge numbers and feels continuous, but size does not matter; anything counted in whole units is discrete.", "traps": {"b": "The number of trees in a forest is a count of whole units, so it is a discrete variable however large the forest is.", "c": "The number of psychology books on a shelf is counted in whole books; there is no value between 12 and 13 books, so it is discrete.", "d": "The number of hairs on a head is a very large count, but it is still a count of whole units and therefore discrete."}}, {"q": "An Ogive represents:", "concept": "The answer is c: an ogive represents a percentile (cumulative) distribution. An ogive is a cumulative frequency curve. To draw it, you add up the frequencies class by class from the bottom (cumulative frequency, cf), usually convert them to cumulative percentages, and plot each against the upper exact limit of its class interval, then join the points with a smooth line. The result rises in an S shape from 0 to 100 per cent. Francis Galton (1875) borrowed the architects' word 'ogive' for this curve. Its main use is to read percentiles and percentile ranks directly: draw a horizontal line from any percentage to the curve and then down to the score axis. The median is the 50th percentile, the first quartile (Q1) the 25th and the third quartile (Q3) the 75th. An ogive also lets two groups be compared across the whole range of scores. Example: from her class ogive, Riya finds that a score of 62 lies at the 80th percentile, so 80 per cent of her classmates scored below 62. Option c is right because the ogive's purpose is to show cumulative percentage, that is, percentile, distributions. Exam trap: a (rank ordered) is tempting because percentiles are a kind of rank, but the ogive plots cumulative frequencies of grouped scores, not a list of ranks; b and d belong to the scatter diagram and the sociogram.", "traps": {"a": "A rank-ordered distribution simply lists cases from highest to lowest; the ogive plots cumulative percentages against score limits, which yields percentiles rather than a ranking.", "b": "Correlated (bivariate) distributions are shown in a scatter diagram or correlation table, with one variable on each axis; the ogive describes a single variable.", "d": "Sociometric distributions of choices and rejections in a group are shown in a sociogram (Jacob Moreno, 1934), not an ogive."}}, {"q": "Which one is not a measure of dispersion?", "concept": "The answer is c: error variable is not a measure of dispersion. A measure of dispersion (variability) tells how spread out scores are around the centre. Two classes can have the same mean of 50, yet in one most scores lie between 45 and 55 while in the other they range from 10 to 90; the average hides this difference, and dispersion reveals it. The standard measures are the range (highest minus lowest score, quick but based on only two scores), the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range ((Q3 minus Q1) / 2, used with the median), the mean deviation (the average of absolute deviations from the mean), the variance (the mean of squared deviations from the mean) and the standard deviation (the square root of the variance, the most stable and widely used, named by Karl Pearson in 1893). The coefficient of variation (SD divided by the mean, times 100) compares relative spread. Example: Arjun's test scores have an SD of 4 and Meera's an SD of 12; Meera's performance is far less consistent, even though both average 60. Option c is right because 'error variable' is not a descriptive statistic of spread at all. Exam trap: the phrase echoes 'error variance', which is real, but error variance is a component of total variance in analysis of variance and in reliability theory (total variance = true variance plus error variance), not a measure of the spread of a set of scores.", "traps": {"a": "The range (highest minus lowest score) is the simplest measure of dispersion, though crude, since it depends only on the two extreme scores.", "b": "The variance, the mean of squared deviations from the mean, is a basic measure of dispersion and the foundation of analysis of variance.", "d": "The standard deviation, the square root of the variance, is the most widely used and most stable measure of dispersion."}}, {"q": "Gifted underachievers can be subjected to pre and post intervention treatment in a:", "concept": "The answer is c: gifted underachievers are best given pre and post intervention treatment in a counselling programme. A gifted underachiever is a student of high ability (high IQ or aptitude) whose school achievement falls well below that ability. Common causes are low motivation, poor study habits, boredom with an unchallenging curriculum, perfectionism and fear of failure, family conflict and emotional problems. Because the causes are personal and emotional, the remedy is a planned counselling programme: individual and group sessions, study skills and goal setting, motivation building, and work with parents and teachers. Such a programme is a treatment, so it fits a one-group pretest-posttest design (Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, 1963): measure achievement, motivation or study habits before the programme, run it, then measure again and compare. For example, Kabir scores 130 on an IQ test but keeps failing mathematics; after twelve weeks of counselling, his study habits score and marks are compared with his baseline. Option c is right because counselling is the structured helping intervention that works on the causes of underachievement and whose results can be measured before and after. Exam trap: guidance and counselling are paired so often that students treat them as one thing. Guidance is the broader, mainly informational service (information, tests and advice for choices); counselling is the intensive, face-to-face helping relationship that works on a problem. Key note: a school guidance programme may include such counselling, so d is tempting; but a treatment with pre and post measures is a counselling intervention, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Face-to-face contact is a feature of every counselling session, not a programme in itself; it names the medium, not a planned intervention with goals and measured outcomes.", "b": "An in-depth interview is an assessment or data-gathering technique used inside counselling or research to explore causes; it diagnoses the problem but is not the treatment that pre and post measures evaluate.", "d": "A guidance programme is the broader school service of information, testing and advice on educational and career choices; it is mainly preventive and informational, not the intensive treatment gifted underachievers need."}}, {"q": "Most important element of a counseling process is:", "concept": "The answer is c: rapport building is the most important element of the counselling process. Rapport is a warm, trusting and accepting relationship between counsellor and client in which the client feels safe, understood and respected. It is built from the first contact: greeting the client warmly, explaining the purpose of the sessions and the limits of confidentiality, starting with neutral topics, listening attentively and accepting the client without judging. Every later stage depends on it. The counselling process usually runs from relationship building through assessment, goal setting and intervention to termination, and without rapport the client will not disclose real problems, accept feedback or follow a plan. Research on the therapeutic alliance points the same way: Edward Bordin (1979) described it as agreement on goals, agreement on tasks and an emotional bond, and the quality of this relationship is one of the most consistent predictors of outcome across therapies. For example, Meera comes to the college counsellor saying only that she 'cannot study'; only in the second session, once she trusts the counsellor, does she mention that her parents are separating. Option c is right because rapport is the foundation on which every other part of counselling rests. Exam trap: structuring sounds technical and important, but it is one early step (explaining the nature, roles, limits and goals of counselling), and it works only once some rapport exists.", "traps": {"a": "Structuring means explaining to the client the nature, roles, time limits and goals of counselling; it is a useful early step, but it organises the relationship rather than creating the trust that makes counselling work.", "b": "Communication, verbal and non-verbal, is the medium through which all counselling happens; it is necessary but not a distinct element, and communication without trust stays guarded.", "d": "Secrecy is not the professional term; the ethical principle is confidentiality, which has limits (risk of harm to self or others, legal duties) and supports rapport rather than replacing it."}}, {"q": "In absence of family counseling which measure is generally adopted in resolution of family conflicts?", "concept": "The answer is a: when there is no family counselling, family conflicts are generally resolved by addressing them to the head of the family. In the traditional Indian family, especially the joint family in which several generations share one household, the head of the family (often the eldest male, called the karta in a Hindu undivided family) or the senior elders act as mediators. Each side presents its case, the head listens, weighs the family's interests and customs, and gives a decision that members are expected to accept out of respect for age and authority. This informal mediation keeps disputes inside the family, protects its reputation and avoids the cost and publicity of outside action. For example, when Arjun and his younger brother Rohan quarrel over who will run the family shop, they take the matter to their grandfather, who divides the work and settles it. Indian clinicians see the same pattern in therapy: families usually accompany patients and share decisions, and Jaswant Singh Neki (1973) proposed the wise, guiding guru-chela relationship as an Indian therapeutic model. Option a is right because referral to the family head is the customary, generally adopted mechanism when professional help is absent. Exam trap: 'compromise' sounds like the mature answer, but giving way on primary (core) issues usually leaves resentment, and in practice even a compromise is usually brokered by the family head. Key note: this item reflects the Indian joint family context, and mediation by elders overlaps with compromise; but referring the matter to the family head is the generally adopted measure, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Fighting among the concerned members is the conflict itself escalating, not a way of resolving it; it hardens positions and damages relationships.", "c": "Going to a court of law is a formal, costly and public last resort for property or marital disputes that cannot be settled at home; it is not the generally adopted first measure.", "d": "Compromise can end minor quarrels, but giving up on primary issues tends to leave resentment so the conflict returns; in Indian families even a compromise is usually arranged through the elders."}}, {"q": "Most important characteristic of a counselor is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the most important characteristic of a counsellor is an empathic attitude. Empathy is the ability to understand the client's feelings and meanings from the client's own frame of reference, as if one were in the client's place without losing the 'as if' quality, and to communicate that understanding back. Carl Rogers (1957), in 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', listed six conditions, three of which (the core conditions) the counsellor must offer: accurate empathic understanding, unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance without conditions) and congruence (genuineness). Counselling texts single out empathy as the counsellor quality most closely tied to the client feeling understood, and process research links it consistently with good outcomes. Robert Carkhuff (1969) built a five-level empathy scale for training helpers, and Gerard Egan (1975) separated primary empathy from advanced empathy. For example, when Riya says 'Nobody at home even notices me', an empathic counsellor replies 'You feel invisible and hurt in your own family', instead of giving advice or defending her parents. Option b is right because empathy is the attitude that makes the client feel understood, and every technique works through it. Exam trap: empathy is not sympathy (feeling sorry for the client), and 'professional skills' looks like the sensible answer, but Rogers argued that techniques without the core attitudes do not produce change. Key note: active listening is a close rival, since empathy is shown through listening, and Rogers later called congruence the most basic condition (not an option here); but listening is the skill and empathy the attitude it expresses, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The ability to listen properly (attending, paraphrasing, reflecting feelings) is the basic counselling skill, but it is the vehicle for empathy, not the core attitude itself.", "c": "Maintaining confidentiality is an ethical duty of every counsellor, with limits such as risk of harm; it protects the relationship but is a professional obligation, not the defining personal characteristic.", "d": "Professional skills (assessment, techniques, case formulation) matter, but Carl Rogers held that skills without empathy, regard and genuineness do not help the client change."}}, {"q": "For resolution of emotional problems which type of counseling can be recommended for better results?", "concept": "The answer is c: for emotional problems, personal counselling is the type that gives better results. Indian guidance texts divide guidance and counselling into three main areas: educational (choice of courses, study problems), vocational (choosing and adjusting to a career) and personal (emotional, social and adjustment problems). Personal counselling deals with the client's feelings and relationships: anxiety, low self-esteem, loneliness, grief, conflict with parents or friends, adjustment to a new college or the end of a relationship. It aims at emotional relief, self-understanding and better adjustment, using rapport, empathic listening, clarification of feelings and problem solving. It is usually individual and confidential, though group formats exist. For example, Kabir has moved from his village to a city hostel; he feels homesick, cries at night and cannot concentrate. His difficulty is emotional, not a lack of career information or a disability, so the counsellor offers personal counselling. Option c is right because personal counselling is the type designed specifically for emotional and adjustment problems. Exam trap: rehabilitation counselling also meets distressed clients, so it tempts; but it is defined by its goal of restoring independent functioning after disability, illness or addiction, not by emotional problems in general.", "traps": {"a": "Appraisal is assessment (testing, ratings, case study) used to understand a client; 'appraisal counselling' is not a standard type, and appraisal alone does not resolve emotional problems.", "b": "Vocational counselling helps with choosing, preparing for and adjusting to a career, in the tradition of Frank Parsons (1909); it addresses occupational decisions, not emotional distress.", "d": "Rehabilitation counselling helps people with disabilities, chronic illness or recovery from addiction return to independent living and work; it is defined by restoring function, not by emotional problems in general."}}, {"q": "An underachiever is one who achieves:", "concept": "The answer is a: an underachiever is one who achieves less than his IQ (measured ability) predicts. An intelligence test estimates how much a student should be capable of learning; an achievement test measures what the student has actually learnt. When the two match, the student is working to capacity. When achievement falls clearly below the level expected from IQ or aptitude, the student is an underachiever; when achievement is higher than the IQ predicts, usually through hard work and strong motivation, the student is an overachiever. Older texts turned this into a ratio. The educational quotient is EQ = educational age / chronological age x 100, and dividing it by the IQ gives the achievement (or accomplishment) quotient, AQ = EQ / IQ x 100, which equals educational age / mental age x 100; the idea was worked out in the 1920s, for example in Raymond Franzen's The Accomplishment Ratio (1922). An AQ below 100 means achievement lags behind ability. Today the same idea appears as the ability-achievement discrepancy. For example, Arjun has an IQ of 125, which predicts top marks, but his achievement scores are only average; he is an underachiever, and the counsellor looks for causes such as poor study habits, low motivation or stress at home. Option a is right because underachievement is defined as achievement falling short of measured ability. Exam trap: AQ already is the achievement-to-ability ratio, so 'less than his AQ' or 'more than his AQ' compares achievement with itself and makes no sense; the comparison must be with IQ.", "traps": {"b": "Achieving more than the IQ predicts defines an overachiever, usually a highly motivated, hard-working student; it is the opposite of underachievement.", "c": "AQ (achievement quotient) is itself the ratio of achievement to ability, so achieving 'less than his AQ' compares achievement with itself; underachievement shows as an AQ below 100, not as achievement below AQ.", "d": "'More than his AQ' is equally circular; an AQ above 100 would point to overachievement, but this option defines neither term."}}, {"q": "CBT targets:", "concept": "The answer is c: cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) targets the perception, that is, the way the client interprets and appraises events. Its central principle, going back to the Stoic philosopher Epictetus (about 50 to 135 CE), is that people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. Aaron Beck noticed around 1960 that depressed patients had quick negative automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions (in his 1960s papers arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation; personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking were added in 1976) rooted in deeper core beliefs, and set out the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). Albert Ellis (1955), founder of what is now called rational emotive behaviour therapy, put the same idea as the ABC model: an Activating event leads to emotional Consequences through the person's Beliefs about it. Change the perception and the emotion and behaviour change with it. In practice, therapist and client identify thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and build more realistic alternatives. For example, Riya fails one test and thinks 'I am stupid and will never pass'; CBT helps her weigh the evidence (she passed the last four tests) and reframe it as 'I did not prepare this topic well', which eases her hopelessness. Option c is right because faulty perception and interpretation of events is the defining target of CBT. Exam trap: the word 'behaviour' in the name pulls students to b, but behaviour change in CBT is largely a means of testing and changing thoughts. Key note: CBT works on both thoughts and behaviour (behavioural experiments, activity scheduling), so b is close; but its defining target is the faulty perception or interpretation, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "'The client' as a whole person is the focus of Carl Rogers's (1951) client-centred therapy, which offers empathy, regard and genuineness rather than targeting specific thoughts.", "b": "Behaviour is the target of behaviour therapy proper (conditioning, exposure, reinforcement, as in Joseph Wolpe's 1958 systematic desensitisation); CBT changes behaviour too, but mainly through the thoughts that drive it.", "d": "Emotion is the direct target of emotion-focused therapy (Leslie Greenberg); in CBT emotions change as a result of changed appraisals, so they are an outcome rather than the target."}}, {"q": "Humanistic counseling is generally attributed to:", "concept": "The answer is b: humanistic counselling is generally attributed to Carl Rogers. Carl Rogers (1942), in Counseling and Psychotherapy, introduced non-directive counselling, and in Client-Centered Therapy (1951) he developed it into client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, the main humanistic approach to counselling. Its assumptions are humanistic: every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive towards growth; problems arise from incongruence between the self-concept and actual experience, created by conditions of worth; and the client, not the counsellor, is the expert on his or her own life. So the counsellor does not diagnose, interpret or advise, but offers three core conditions: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). Given these, the client's own growth forces take over. Abraham Maslow (Motivation and Personality, 1954) is the other founder of humanistic psychology, but as a theorist of motivation rather than of counselling. For example, when Kabir cannot decide between engineering and music, a Rogerian counsellor reflects his feelings ('You feel torn between what your family expects and what you love') and trusts him to reach his own decision. Option b is right because Rogers created the humanistic, client-centred approach to counselling. Exam trap: the 'talking cure' of Josef Breuer's patient Anna O. sounds like counselling, and Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis on it, but both names belong to psychoanalysis; humanistic psychology arose as the 'third force', against both psychoanalysis and behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) founded psychoanalysis, which interprets unconscious conflicts in a deterministic way; humanistic psychology arose as a reaction against it.", "c": "William Thompson is a filler name with no link to humanistic counselling; no major counselling approach is attributed to him.", "d": "Josef Breuer was Freud's mentor, whose patient Anna O. led to the cathartic method (Studies on Hysteria, 1895, with Freud); that is a root of psychoanalysis, not of humanistic counselling."}}, {"q": "Vocational guidance emphasizes:", "concept": "The answer is c: vocational guidance emphasises fitting the individual's appraisal data to the requirements of the job market. Frank Parsons (Choosing a Vocation, 1909), the father of vocational guidance, set out three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests, ambitions and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions, pay and prospects of different lines of work; and (3) 'true reasoning' on the relations between these two groups of facts. The third step, matching the person to the job, is the heart of the process. Edmund G. Williamson (How to Counsel Students, 1939) developed this into the trait-and-factor approach: measure the person's traits with aptitude tests, interest inventories and school records, describe the factors each job demands, and match the two. Later career theories kept the same idea of person-job fit. For example, Meera's tests show high clerical speed, strong numerical ability and orderly, conventional interests; the counsellor matches this profile to openings in banking and accounts rather than simply reporting her scores. Option c is right because vocational guidance exists to match appraised abilities and interests to occupational demands. Exam trap: options a, b and d are each one ingredient of Parsons's process; the stem asks for the emphasis, which is the step that joins them. Key note: d (providing occupational information) is part of vocational guidance (Parsons's step 2), but the emphasis is on matching the person to the job, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Personal appraisal (testing aptitudes, interests and personality) is only Parsons's first step, knowing the self; on its own it gives a profile but no vocational decision.", "b": "Strengths and weakness analysis is another name for the same first step, self-knowledge; it does not connect the person to actual occupations.", "d": "Providing occupational information is Parsons's second step, knowledge of the world of work; information alone does not guide until it is matched to the person's abilities and interests."}}, {"q": "Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing:", "concept": "The answer is b: Bhatia's Battery is used for assessing intelligence. C. M. Bhatia (1955) built the Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence for Indian conditions, including people who cannot read. It has five performance subtests: Kohs block design (copying coloured designs with cubes), Alexander's pass-along test (sliding blocks in a tray into a target pattern), pattern drawing, immediate memory for digits, and picture construction (assembling cut pieces of pictures). Because it needs no reading and little language, and was standardised with separate norms for literate and illiterate children aged 11 to 16, it is used with illiterate and less schooled people and minimises the educational bias of verbal tests. The subtest scores are combined into one Performance Quotient (PQ), Bhatia's own name for it, as distinct from an IQ. It stands beside other Indian intelligence tests such as the Binet-Kamat Test (V. V. Kamat, 1930s) and Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (Arthur Malin, 1969). For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun, a 12-year-old from a village with little schooling, chooses Bhatia's Battery over the WISC, which leans on schooling and foreign norms. Option b is right because the battery is a non-verbal performance test of general intelligence. Exam trap: because its subtests look like practical, manipulative tasks, students mistake it for an aptitude test, but it yields one intelligence score, not a profile of specific aptitudes.", "traps": {"a": "Personality is assessed by inventories (the EPI, 16PF, MMPI) and projective tests (Rorschach, TAT); Bhatia's subtests are timed problem-solving tasks, not measures of traits.", "c": "Attitudes are measured by Thurstone, Likert or Bogardus scales of agreement with statements; the battery contains no attitude items.", "d": "Aptitude tests measure specific potentials (the eight subtests of the Differential Aptitude Tests, the Minnesota Clerical Test); Bhatia's Battery, although made of performance tasks, gives one general intelligence score."}}, {"q": "Measurement by definition is a process of:", "concept": "The answer is a: by definition, measurement is a process of assigning scores (numbers) to qualities or attributes. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in 'On the theory of scales of measurement', defined measurement as the assignment of numerals to objects or events according to rules. In psychology the things measured are attributes of people: intelligence, anxiety, attitude, reaction time. A test or rating scale supplies the rule, so that a quality we cannot see directly becomes a quantity we can compare, such as an IQ of 110 or an anxiety score of 32. Stevens also showed that the rule fixes the level of measurement: nominal (numbers as labels), ordinal (rank order), interval (equal units, arbitrary zero) and ratio (equal units, true zero), and each level allows different statistics. Measurement is kept apart from evaluation, which judges a score against a standard (42 is a pass), and from assessment, which combines many sources to answer a question about a person. For example, when Riya's teacher rates her classroom confidence from 1 to 5, she is assigning a number to a qualitative attribute by a rule: that is measurement. Option a is right because it matches the textbook definition of measurement as assigning numbers to attributes. Exam trap: b (describing data in numbers) and c (applying statistics) describe what happens after measurement, not measurement itself. Key note: the options are loosely worded, and b and d also hint at quantifying; a is the one that matches the textbook definition of assigning numbers to attributes, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Describing collections of information in numbers is descriptive statistics (tables, means, percentages), which summarises data that measurement has already produced.", "c": "Extending statistical treatment to research information is data analysis, the stage after measurement, when scores are tested and interpreted.", "d": "Planning how to turn qualities into quantities describes designing a measuring instrument (test construction), not the act of measurement, which actually assigns the numbers."}}, {"q": "For effective sampling an important condition is:", "concept": "The answer is d: an important condition for effective sampling is knowing the structure of the group (the population) to be sampled. A sample is useful only if it represents its population, and a representative sample cannot be drawn without knowing what the population is made of: its size, its boundaries (who belongs), how homogeneous or heterogeneous it is, and which subgroups (strata or clusters) it contains. This knowledge drives every later decision. A homogeneous population can be represented by a small simple random sample; a heterogeneous one needs a larger sample, and if it has clear subgroups, stratified random sampling draws from each subgroup in proportion to its size. Natural groupings such as schools or villages allow cluster sampling. Research texts list the essentials of a good sample as representativeness, adequate size, independence of selection and freedom from bias, and all of these depend on the population's structure. For example, Meera wants to survey stress in a university that is 60% women, has arts, science and commerce faculties, and has both day scholars and hostellers; knowing this structure, she stratifies by gender and faculty so that every group is fairly represented. Option d is right because the structure of the group decides the sample size, the sampling method and whether the sample can stand for the whole. Exam trap: 'existence of a group' sounds basic and therefore important, but every sample presupposes a population; mere existence says nothing about how to sample it. Key note: a sound sampling procedure (random selection) also matters, but the procedure is chosen to fit the structure of the group, so the structure is the prior condition, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The existence of a group (a population) is presupposed by any sample; it is necessary but tells the researcher nothing about how to draw a representative sample.", "b": "The sampling procedure (simple random, stratified, cluster, quota) matters, but it is chosen to fit the structure of the population, so it depends on d.", "c": "The research focus (the problem and hypotheses) decides what is studied and which population is relevant, but it is not a condition of the sampling itself."}}, {"q": "Chi-square analysis is relevant for:", "concept": "The answer is b: chi-square analysis is relevant for nonparametric data, that is, frequency counts in categories. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test (χ²) to compare the observed frequency (O) in each category with the frequency expected (E) under the null hypothesis: χ² = Σ (O - E)² / E. It is used in two main ways: the goodness-of-fit test (do the counts on one variable match an expected pattern?) and the test of independence in a contingency table (are two categorical variables related?). Chi-square is nonparametric, or distribution-free: it assumes no normal population and works with nominal (or ordinal) data, where there are no means or standard deviations, only counts. It needs raw frequencies (not percentages), each person in only one cell, and expected frequencies of about 5 or more per cell. Parametric tests (t, F, Pearson r), by contrast, assume interval or ratio scores from normal populations with similar variances. For example, Kabir asks 100 students whether they prefer online or classroom teaching and notes each student's gender; a 2 x 2 chi-square tells him whether preference is related to gender. Option b is right because chi-square is a nonparametric test for categorical frequency data. Exam trap: chi-square can be run on survey or ex post facto data, so c and d look possible, but those words name research designs, not kinds of data; what makes chi-square relevant is that the data are frequencies, whatever the design.", "traps": {"a": "Parametric data are interval or ratio scores from normally distributed populations, analysed with t-tests, ANOVA and Pearson r; chi-square makes no such assumptions.", "c": "A one-shot survey is a design that collects data once from one sample; chi-square fits it only when its results are counts in categories, and the same survey could yield scores for a t-test.", "d": "Ex post facto (after the fact) observational data come from a design that compares existing groups without manipulation; the design does not decide the test, the type of data does."}}, {"q": "Reliability of a test is contingent upon:", "concept": "The answer is b: the reliability of a test is contingent upon its length. Reliability is the consistency of scores: a reliable test gives nearly the same result when a person is retested or given an equivalent form. Every observed score is a true score plus random error. Each item samples the trait with some error; when more parallel items are added, the random errors tend to cancel out while the true score accumulates, so a longer test is more reliable. The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula, published separately by Charles Spearman (1910) and William Brown (1910), predicts the effect: r new = n r / (1 + (n - 1) r), where n is the number of times the test is lengthened. If Ishaan's 20-item anxiety scale has a reliability of 0.60, doubling it to 40 comparable items gives 2(0.60) / (1 + 0.60) = 0.75. The same formula steps a split-half correlation up to full-test length. Gains shrink as the test grows, and the added items must measure the same trait. Other influences are group heterogeneity (a wide range raises r), item difficulty and discrimination, guessing, objectivity of scoring, speededness and the retest interval. Option b is right because test length is the classic determinant of reliability, with its own formula. Exam trap: objectivity concerns scorer agreement, only one source of error. Key note: objectivity of scoring and the retest interval also affect reliability, but test length is the factor the textbooks put first, with its own formula, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The 'nature of the test' is too vague to be a determinant; the kind of test (speed or power, objective or essay) matters only through specific factors such as length, scoring and speededness.", "c": "Objectivity of scoring is a real influence, since objective keys remove scorer error, but it addresses one source of error only and is not what reliability chiefly depends on.", "d": "The retesting interval affects only test-retest coefficients (longer intervals lower them); it is a feature of one way of estimating reliability, not of the test itself."}}, {"q": "Construct validity of an instrument is appropriate for the situations where:", "concept": "The answer is d: construct validity is appropriate where no other form of validity is applicable, that is, where there is no adequate criterion or content domain. A construct is a theoretical, unobservable attribute such as anxiety, intelligence, creativity or ego strength. Content validity works when the domain can be listed and sampled (a spelling test of class 5 words); criterion-related validity works when there is an outside measure to predict (job ratings, exam marks). For many psychological traits there is neither: no single agreed criterion of 'anxiety' exists. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), in 'Construct validity in psychological tests', argued that construct validity must be investigated whenever no criterion or universe of content is accepted as entirely adequate to define the quality being measured. The test is then judged by whether its scores behave as the theory (the nomological network) predicts: convergent and discriminant correlations (Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959, the multitrait-multimethod matrix), factor analysis, group differences and change after an experimental intervention. For example, Riya builds a resilience scale; with no criterion of 'true resilience', she shows that it correlates highly with an existing resilience measure, weakly with intelligence, and rises after resilience training. Option d is right because construct validity is the approach used when criterion and content validity cannot settle the question. Exam trap: b (concepts reduced to numbers) is just measurement, and c (content-based formats) points to content validity. Key note: a ('access to concepts') also points at constructs, since a construct is a theoretical concept; but the textbook condition for using construct validity, from Cronbach and Meehl, is the lack of an adequate criterion or content domain, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Access to concepts' gestures at constructs, but every test aims at some concept; the defining condition for construct validation is the absence of an adequate criterion or content domain.", "b": "Reducing concepts to numbers is measurement itself (assigning numbers by rule), which every test does whatever kind of validity it relies on.", "c": "Tests whose formats are only content based, such as achievement tests sampling a syllabus, are validated by content validity, judged by experts against a table of specifications."}}, {"q": "Item analysis of a psychological scale helps in:", "concept": "The answer is b: item analysis helps in eliminating the weaker items of a psychological scale. After a pool of items is written and tried out on a sample, item analysis examines each item statistically to decide which to keep, revise or discard. The difficulty index (p) is the proportion of examinees who pass or endorse the item; items that almost everyone passes or fails tell no one apart, so most tests keep p between about 0.30 and 0.70. The discrimination index (D) compares upper and lower scorers on the total test, usually the top and bottom 27 percent, the split Truman Kelley (1939) showed works best; D = p upper - p lower. Items with low or negative D, or low item-total correlations, fail to separate high from low scorers, and a negative D usually means a miskeyed or ambiguous item. Distractor analysis checks that each wrong option attracts some low scorers. Weak items are then revised or dropped, and keeping only the good ones raises the reliability and validity of the final form. For example, Kabir tries out 60 items for an exam anxiety scale on 200 students; 12 items have D below 0.20 and two have negative D, so he discards them and keeps 46. Option b is right because the direct purpose of item analysis is to find and remove or revise weak items. Exam trap: c is tempting because better items do improve validity, but item analysis does not predict a validity coefficient; validity must be established separately against a criterion or a construct.", "traps": {"a": "Test length is set by the content plan, the reliability needed (the Spearman-Brown formula) and practical time limits; item analysis may shorten a test, but it does not decide its length.", "c": "Validity is established separately through content, criterion-related or construct validation; item analysis improves the item pool but does not predict the test's validity coefficient.", "d": "Comparing results with existing instruments is a validation step (concurrent or convergent validity) carried out on the finished test, not part of analysing single items."}}, {"q": "Which is the major difficulty in personality assessment?", "concept": "The answer is c: the major difficulty in personality assessment is the dynamic status of personality. Gordon Allport (1937) defined personality as 'the dynamic organization within the individual of those psychophysical systems that determine his unique adjustments to his environment'. Dynamic means personality is not a fixed object: it keeps growing and changing with age and experience, it is an active interplay of motives, emotions, habits and beliefs, and it shows itself differently as the person adjusts to different situations. Any test or interview therefore captures only a snapshot of a moving, organised whole, and the same person may look different next year or in another setting; because personality is also complex and unique, no single instrument captures all of it. Other difficulties follow: inventories invite faking and socially desirable answers, projective tests depend on subjective interpretation, and Walter Mischel (1968, Personality and Assessment) found that trait scores predict behaviour in any one situation only weakly, with correlations rarely much above 0.30. For example, Meera scores as shy on an inventory in her first college month, yet a year later she leads the debating society. Option c is right because the dynamic, changing nature of personality is the root problem behind the other difficulties. Exam trap: subjectivity is a weakness of particular methods, not of personality itself. Key note: subjectivity and behavioural variability are real difficulties too (Mischel's critique is the famous one); the item treats the dynamic, ever-changing status of personality as the root problem, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Patterned individuality means each person's traits form a unique, organised pattern (Allport's idiographic view); uniqueness complicates comparison, but the pattern is fairly stable and is exactly what assessment tries to capture.", "b": "Extreme subjectivity is a weakness of particular methods (interviews, projective tests such as the Rorschach) that objective inventories reduce; it is a problem of the tools, not the central difficulty of personality itself.", "d": "Behavioural variability across situations is Walter Mischel's (1968) famous critique, but it is one expression of personality's dynamic, situation-sensitive nature, which option c names more broadly."}}, {"q": "Structured testing situations are itemized in:", "concept": "The answer is c: structured testing situations are itemised in the EPI. A structured test presents clear, specific items with fixed response options and objective scoring, so answers can be counted against a key and compared with norms. The Eysenck Personality Inventory (EPI), published by Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1964), has 57 yes/no questions about sociability, impulsiveness, moods and worries. They are scored for extraversion (E), neuroticism (N) and a Lie scale (L) that catches faking good. It followed the Maudsley Personality Inventory (1959) and was later replaced by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (EPQ, 1975), which added psychoticism. The other three options are projective techniques, which are deliberately unstructured: an ambiguous stimulus, minimal instructions and free responses, on the projective hypothesis that the person projects needs and conflicts into whatever structure he or she imposes on the material. For example, Arjun answers the 57 yes/no items of the EPI and receives objective E and N scores in one short sitting, whereas on the TAT he tells free stories that a trained examiner must interpret. Option c is right because the EPI is an objective, itemised inventory with fixed questions and fixed answers. Exam trap: RIT is not a familiar abbreviation; it is the Rorschach Inkblot Test, the most unstructured test of all, so do not guess that an unfamiliar set of initials must be an inventory.", "traps": {"a": "The TAT (Thematic Apperception Test, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) shows ambiguous pictures and asks for stories; it is an unstructured projective technique interpreted for needs and press.", "b": "RIT is the Rorschach Inkblot Test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921): ten inkblots and the open question of what each might be, the classic unstructured projective test.", "d": "The CAT (Children's Apperception Test, Leopold and Sonya Bellak, 1949) is a children's version of the TAT with animal pictures for ages 3 to 10; it is projective and unstructured."}}, {"q": "Aptitude by definition is nearer to:", "concept": "The answer is a: by definition, aptitude is nearest to Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) concept of primary mental abilities (PMA). Walter Bingham (1937, Aptitudes and Aptitude Testing) defined an aptitude as a condition or set of characteristics regarded as symptomatic of a person's ability to acquire, with training, some specific knowledge, skill or set of responses. Two ideas matter: aptitudes are specific (separate abilities such as mechanical, clerical, musical or numerical), and they are potentials that predict future learning. Thurstone, using multiple factor analysis, rejected a single dominant general factor and found seven fairly independent primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. This idea of several separate abilities is the basis of the multiple aptitude batteries, such as the Differential Aptitude Tests (George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman, 1947) and the General Aptitude Test Battery (1947), which give a profile of separate scores rather than one IQ. For example, Kabir's profile shows high space and mechanical reasoning scores but average verbal ability; a counsellor reads this as an aptitude for engineering drawing rather than law. Option a is right because aptitude, like Thurstone's PMAs, means specific, separable abilities. Exam trap: Charles Spearman's (1904) specific factors (s) sound like aptitudes, but they are narrow, task-bound factors, and his theory stresses g, general intelligence. Key note: Meier's test is an aptitude test, so c is tempting; but the stem asks which concept the definition of aptitude is nearest to, and that is the PMA idea of specific abilities, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman's two-factor theory (1904, 1927) stresses g, a general intelligence common to all tasks, plus task-specific s factors; it is a theory of general intelligence, the opposite emphasis to aptitude.", "c": "The Meier Art Judgment Test (Norman Meier, first published with Carl Seashore in 1929) is a special aptitude test for art; it is an example of an aptitude measure, not a concept that defines aptitude.", "d": "Tandon's GMAT is R. K. Tandon's Group Test of General Mental Ability, an Indian group intelligence test; it measures general mental ability, not specific aptitudes."}}, {"q": "Which item can best fit in a clerical aptitude test?", "concept": "The answer is c: alphabetical filing is the item that best fits a clerical aptitude test. Clerical aptitude is the potential to learn office work quickly and accurately, and its tests stress perceptual speed and accuracy with names and numbers. The classic is the Minnesota Clerical Test of Dorothy Andrew, Donald Paterson and Howard Longstaff (1933): 200 pairs of numbers and 200 pairs of names, and the examinee marks, at speed, the pairs that are exactly the same; the score is right answers minus wrong ones. Clerical batteries add alphabetising and filing (putting names into their correct place in an alphabetical list), coding, copying, checking spelling and simple arithmetic, and the Differential Aptitude Tests include a clerical speed and accuracy subtest. These tasks are small samples of real office work, which is why they predict success in it; in terms of Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) primary mental abilities they draw chiefly on perceptual speed. For example, Riya is asked to insert 'Mehta, R.' and 19 other names into an alphabetical list as fast as she can; her speed and accuracy show how quickly she would learn to file records in an office. Option c is right because alphabetical filing is a direct sample of clerical work, testing speed and accuracy with verbal material. Exam trap: 'discrimination' items sound like checking tasks, but time and patch (colour) discrimination are sensory judgements used in musical or perceptual tests, not clerical checking of names and numbers.", "traps": {"a": "Time discrimination (judging which of two intervals is longer) is a sensory task in Carl Seashore's Measures of Musical Talents (1919), a musical aptitude test, not a clerical one.", "b": "Total memory is not a clerical task; memory tests (digit span, associative memory) measure retention, whereas clerical tests measure speed and accuracy on material in front of the examinee.", "d": "Patch discrimination (telling apart patches of colour or brightness) is a visual sensory task used in colour vision or perception testing, unrelated to office work."}}]});
